module.exports = { ID: 'AQA-8652', KITS: {"3.1.1":{"id":"AQA-8652","topic":"3.1.1","family":"language","lesson":{"why":"Theme 1 covers identity and relationships, healthy living, and education and work — the three areas of your own life you are most often asked to describe, compare and give opinions on. Talking about people needs adjectives that agree and comparisons with plus … que; talking about habits needs frequency phrases, reflexive verbs and negation; talking about school and the future needs il faut, on ne peut pas and aller + infinitive. Getting these structures secure lets you say what you actually think about your own life, not just recite set phrases.","idea":[{"code":"Topic 1","h":"Identity and relationships with others","t":"This topic covers describing yourself and the people close to you: physical description, personality, and family and friendship relationships. Build a description in three layers — who someone is, what they are like, and why the relationship matters: Ma meilleure amie s'appelle Léa ; elle est très drôle et elle me fait toujours rire (My best friend is called Léa; she is very funny and she always makes me laugh). Personality adjectives agree with the person: sportif/sportive, gentil/gentille, travailleur/travailleuse. Comparisons are constant in this topic: Mon frère est plus grand que moi, mais je suis plus patient que lui (My brother is taller than me, but I am more patient than him) — after que you need the emphatic pronoun (moi, toi, lui, elle). Relationship verbs are often reflexive: je m'entends bien avec ma sœur (I get on well with my sister), nous nous disputons parfois (we sometimes argue). Prepare true, specific detail about your own family and friends rather than generic sentences."},{"code":"Topic 2","h":"Healthy living and lifestyle","t":"This topic covers diet, exercise, sleep and habits, good and bad, and giving opinions with reasons about them. State a habit, then evaluate it: Je mange beaucoup de légumes et je fais du sport deux fois par semaine, donc je suis en bonne santé (I eat a lot of vegetables and do sport twice a week, so I am in good health). After a negative verb and after expressions of quantity the article shrinks to de: je ne mange pas de viande (I don't eat meat), trop de sucre (too much sugar). Negative habits use ne … jamais and ne … pas: Je ne fume jamais (I never smoke). Daily routine is built from reflexive verbs: je me lève à sept heures, je me couche tard (I get up at seven, I go to bed late). il faut + infinitive gives general advice: Il faut dormir huit heures (You have to sleep eight hours). Give a genuine opinion with a reason whenever this topic comes up — c'est bon pour la santé parce que … — not just a description of a habit."},{"code":"Topic 3","h":"Education and work","t":"This topic covers school subjects and opinions of them, rules and pressures at school, and part-time jobs or future career plans. Give a subject opinion with a reason: Je préfère les sciences parce que le prof explique tout très clairement (I prefer science because the teacher explains everything very clearly). School rules use il faut and on ne peut pas: Il faut porter un uniforme et on ne peut pas utiliser son portable en classe (You have to wear a uniform and you cannot use your phone in class). Future plans use aller + infinitive, often after a condition with si + present tense: Si je réussis mes examens, je vais aller au lycée ; sinon, je vais chercher un travail (If I pass my exams, I am going to go to sixth form; if not, I am going to look for a job). Work vocabulary — un apprentissage (an apprenticeship), un petit boulot (a part-time job), gagner de l'argent (to earn money) — lets you explain what you want to do and why."}],"examples":[{"title":"Describing a friend with a comparison","setup":"Translate into French (the speaker is a boy): 'My sister is taller than me, but I am more patient than her.'","steps":["'Taller than me' uses plus + adjective + que: plus grande que moi — grande takes -e to agree with sœur.","After que, 'me' is the emphatic pronoun moi, not je.","'More patient than her' follows the same pattern: plus patient qu'elle — patient stays masculine for the boy speaking, and que shortens to qu' before elle.","Full sentence: Ma sœur est plus grande que moi, mais je suis plus patient qu'elle."],"cues":["Which structure means 'more … than', and does the adjective need to agree?","Which form of 'me' follows que?","Does patient agree with the speaker or with the sister, and what happens to que before elle?","What is the complete sentence?"]},{"title":"Translating a healthy-living text","setup":"Translate into English: 'Je mange beaucoup de fruits et de légumes, et je fais du sport trois fois par semaine. Pourtant, je ne dors pas assez pendant la semaine parce que j'ai trop de devoirs.'","steps":["The first sentence gives two habits: je mange beaucoup de fruits et de légumes (I eat a lot of fruit and vegetables) and je fais du sport trois fois par semaine (I do sport three times a week).","pourtant signals a contrast: 'however'.","je ne dors pas assez pendant la semaine is 'I don't sleep enough during the week', and parce que j'ai trop de devoirs gives the reason: 'because I have too much homework'.","Full translation: I eat a lot of fruit and vegetables, and I do sport three times a week. However, I don't sleep enough during the week because I have too much homework."],"cues":["What two habits are named in the first sentence?","What does pourtant signal is coming?","What is the problem with sleep, and what reason is given?","Give the complete English translation."]},{"title":"School rules with il faut and on ne peut pas","setup":"Translate into French: 'At my school you have to arrive on time, and you cannot eat in class.'","steps":["'You have to' in a general sense is il faut + infinitive: il faut arriver.","'On time' is the set phrase à l'heure: il faut arriver à l'heure.","'You cannot' is on ne peut pas + infinitive: on ne peut pas manger en classe.","Full sentence, with the place phrase first: Dans mon collège, il faut arriver à l'heure et on ne peut pas manger en classe."],"cues":["Which impersonal phrase means 'you have to', and what verb form follows it?","How is 'on time' expressed?","Which phrase means 'you cannot'?","Where does 'at my school' go, and how are the halves joined?"]},{"title":"Future plans with si + present and aller + infinitive","setup":"Translate into French: 'If I pass my exams, I am going to study science; if not, I am going to look for a job.'","steps":["'If I pass' uses si with the present tense: si je réussis mes examens.","The result uses aller + infinitive: je vais étudier les sciences.","'If not' is sinon, and its result also uses aller + infinitive: je vais chercher un travail.","Full sentence: Si je réussis mes examens, je vais étudier les sciences ; sinon, je vais chercher un travail."],"cues":["Which tense follows si here?","Which structure gives 'I am going to' + verb?","How do you say 'if not', and what follows it?","What is the complete sentence?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Give the French for 'more … than' and 'less … than'.","a":"plus … que; moins … que."},{"q":"What happens to the article after a negative verb or an expression of quantity?","a":"It becomes de (or d'): je ne mange pas de viande; trop de devoirs."},{"q":"Which structure gives 'I am going to' + verb?","a":"aller + infinitive: je vais étudier."},{"q":"Give the French for 'you cannot' + verb, using on.","a":"on ne peut pas + infinitive."}]},"room":{"facts":["Personality adjectives agree with the person described: sportif/sportive, gentil/gentille, travailleur/travailleuse, drôle (no change).","Comparison: plus/moins + adjective + que (more/less … than); aussi + adjective + que (as … as); after que use the emphatic pronoun (moi, toi, lui, elle).","After a negative verb and after expressions of quantity, the article becomes de: je ne bois pas de café; beaucoup de fruits; trop de devoirs.","School rules: il faut + infinitive (you have to); on ne peut pas + infinitive (you cannot).","Future plans: aller + infinitive (je vais étudier), often after si + present tense (si je réussis, je vais …)."],"questions":[{"q":"Translate into English: 'Ma sœur est plus travailleuse que moi.'","a":"My sister is more hard-working than me.","sol":"My sister is more hard-working than me. plus travailleuse que moi is the 'more … than' comparison; travailleuse is the feminine of travailleur, agreeing with sœur; moi is the emphatic pronoun after que.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["plus … que is a comparison — which way does it point?","Which family member is described, and is the adjective feminine to match?","One complete English sentence keeping the comparison."],"codes":["Topic 1"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'I eat a lot of vegetables every day.'","a":"Je mange beaucoup de légumes tous les jours.","sol":"Je mange beaucoup de légumes tous les jours. beaucoup is an expression of quantity, so it is followed by de with no article; tous les jours is the set phrase for every day.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["What is the je form of manger?","What follows an expression of quantity — an article, or just de?","Verb, quantity phrase + noun, then the time phrase."],"codes":["Topic 2"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'You have to wear a uniform at my school.'","a":"Il faut porter un uniforme dans mon collège.","sol":"Il faut porter un uniforme dans mon collège. il faut + infinitive is the impersonal you have to; dans mon collège gives the place.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Which impersonal phrase means 'you have to' in general?","What form does the verb after that phrase take?","Impersonal phrase, infinitive, object, then the place phrase."],"codes":["Topic 3"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'I get on well with my brother.'","a":"Je m'entends bien avec mon frère.","sol":"Je m'entends bien avec mon frère. s'entendre bien avec is reflexive: the je form is je m'entends, with me shortened to m' before the vowel.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["'Get on with' is a reflexive verb in French — which one?","Check what a reflexive pronoun does before a verb starting with a vowel.","Pronoun + verb + bien + avec + the person."],"codes":["Topic 1"]},{"q":"Read this text and answer in English: 'Mes parents sont très stricts : je ne peux pas sortir pendant la semaine et je dois faire mes devoirs avant le dîner. Pourtant, le week-end, je suis assez libre.' (a) Give one thing the writer cannot do. (b) What is different at the weekend?","a":"(a) They cannot go out during the week. (b) They are fairly free at the weekend.","sol":"(a) je ne peux pas sortir pendant la semaine — I can't go out during the week (the only prohibition in the text; having to do homework is an obligation, not something forbidden). (b) le week-end, je suis assez libre — at the weekend I am fairly free. pourtant (however) marks the contrast.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Look at the sentence before pourtant for what is not allowed.","pourtant signals a change — read what follows it for the second answer.","Two short English answers: (a) one restriction, (b) how the weekend differs."],"codes":["Topic 1"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'Even if I don't like sport, I know it is important for my health.'","a":"Même si je n'aime pas le sport, je sais que c'est important pour ma santé.","sol":"Même si je n'aime pas le sport, je sais que c'est important pour ma santé. même si introduces the concession; the article stays after aimer in the negative (je n'aime pas le sport), because with verbs of liking the definite article is kept; je sais que + clause.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Which two-word phrase means 'even if'?","Check what verbs of liking do to the noun after them, even in the negative.","Concession clause, comma, then je sais que + the main clause."],"codes":["Topic 2"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'In my opinion, homework is useful, but sometimes we have too much homework.'","a":"À mon avis, les devoirs sont utiles, mais parfois nous avons trop de devoirs.","sol":"À mon avis, les devoirs sont utiles, mais parfois nous avons trop de devoirs. devoirs is plural, so sont and utiles agree; trop is an expression of quantity followed by de.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Is devoirs singular or plural in French, and what does that do to the verb and adjective?","What follows 'too much' — an article, or de?","Opinion phrase, subject + verb + adjective, mais, then subject + verb + quantity phrase."],"codes":["Topic 3"]},{"q":"Read this text and answer in English: 'L'année prochaine, si j'ai de bonnes notes, je vais aller à l'université pour étudier la médecine. Sinon, je vais chercher un apprentissage dans un hôpital, parce que j'adore aider les gens.' Explain both of the writer's possible plans and the reason given for the second one.","a":"If they get good marks they are going to university to study medicine; if not, they are going to look for an apprenticeship in a hospital, because they love helping people.","sol":"si j'ai de bonnes notes, je vais aller à l'université pour étudier la médecine — if I get good marks, I am going to go to university to study medicine; sinon, je vais chercher un apprentissage dans un hôpital — if not, I am going to look for an apprenticeship in a hospital; parce que j'adore aider les gens — because I love helping people.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The text gives two possible futures, joined by si and sinon.","Find the reason clause at the very end, introduced by parce que.","Give both plans and the reason, all in English."],"codes":["Topic 3"]},{"q":"Translate into French (the speaker is a girl): 'My grandmother is as generous as she is patient, and she has looked after me since I was small.'","a":"Ma grand-mère est aussi généreuse que patiente, et elle s'occupe de moi depuis que je suis petite.","sol":"Ma grand-mère est aussi généreuse que patiente, et elle s'occupe de moi depuis que je suis petite. aussi … que makes the equal comparison between two adjectives about the same person, both feminine; s'occuper de is 'to look after'; depuis + present tense expresses something that started in the past and continues now, so French uses the present (elle s'occupe) where English uses 'has looked after'.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Which comparison structure says two qualities are equally true of one person?","Which tense does French use after depuis for something still going on?","One sentence: the comparison, then et, then the present-tense clause ending with a 'since' clause with its own verb."],"codes":["Topic 1"]},{"q":"Write a paragraph of about 90 words in French about your lifestyle and future plans, covering: (1) a healthy or unhealthy habit and your opinion of it; (2) a school rule you have to follow; (3) what you are going to do if you pass your exams and what you are going to do if you do not.","a":"About 90 words covering: a habit with an opinion (e.g. je fais du sport…, c'est bon pour la santé), a school rule with il faut or on ne peut pas, and si + present + je vais for both outcomes.","sol":"Je mène une vie assez saine parce que je fais du sport trois fois par semaine et je mange beaucoup de fruits. Pourtant, le week-end, je mange parfois trop de fast-food. Dans mon collège, il faut porter un uniforme et on ne peut pas utiliser son portable en classe ; je trouve ça un peu strict, mais je comprends les règles. Si je réussis mes examens cet été, je vais étudier les sciences au lycée. Sinon, je vais chercher un apprentissage dans un hôpital, parce que j'adore aider les gens. (90 words: habit + opinion, a school rule with il faut / on ne peut pas, and both si + present + aller + infinitive outcomes.)","m":15,"d":3,"hints":["Which structures from this room express a habit, an obligation and a condition?","The school-rule bullet needs an impersonal structure.","Three short parts in the order of the bullets, about 90 words in all."],"codes":["Topic 1","Topic 2","Topic 3"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'Even if my brother and I argue sometimes, we get on well in general, and I know I can always count on him.'","a":"Même si mon frère et moi nous disputons parfois, nous nous entendons bien en général, et je sais que je peux toujours compter sur lui.","sol":"Même si mon frère et moi nous disputons parfois, nous nous entendons bien en général, et je sais que je peux toujours compter sur lui. se disputer and s'entendre are reflexive, with the plural pronoun nous; compter sur takes the emphatic pronoun lui after the preposition.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Which reflexive verbs express 'argue' and 'get on'?","'Count on' needs a preposition, and the pronoun after a preposition is an emphatic one — which?","Concession clause, main clause, then et + je sais que + clause."],"codes":["Topic 1"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'I try to sleep enough during the week, but sometimes I go to bed very late because I have too much homework.'","a":"J'essaie de dormir assez pendant la semaine, mais parfois je me couche très tard parce que j'ai trop de devoirs.","sol":"J'essaie de dormir assez pendant la semaine, mais parfois je me couche très tard parce que j'ai trop de devoirs. essayer de + infinitive is 'to try to'; se coucher is reflexive, 'to go to bed'; trop de + noun with no article.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Which verb means 'to try to', and which preposition links it to the next verb?","Which reflexive verb means 'to go to bed'?","One sentence with two clauses joined by mais, the second ending with a reason clause."],"codes":["Topic 2","Topic 3"]},{"q":"You are describing a photo to your teacher: it shows a family eating dinner together in a kitchen. Write five short sentences in French about the photo.","a":"Five short sentences, each with a verb: who is there, where, what they are doing, an object, a feeling (e.g. Il y a une famille. Ils mangent dans la cuisine.).","sol":"Five short, correct sentences each giving one detail: who is there (il y a une famille / quatre personnes), where (ils sont dans la cuisine), what they are doing (ils mangent, la mère parle), an object (il y a du pain sur la table) and a feeling or opinion (ils sont contents / c'est sympa). Each sentence needs a verb.","m":10,"d":2,"hints":["Which structure says what there is in a picture?","Give one detail per sentence: people, place, action, object, feeling.","Five separate short sentences, each with its own verb."],"codes":["Topic 1"]},{"q":"Translate into English: 'Je fais du sport trois fois par semaine, mais je ne bois jamais d'eau.'","a":"I do sport three times a week, but I never drink water.","sol":"I do sport three times a week, but I never drink water. faire du sport is 'to do sport'; ne … jamais is 'never', and after the negative the partitive shrinks to d' before eau.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Look for the two-part negative around the second verb.","trois fois par semaine is a frequency phrase — what does it mean?","One complete English sentence with two clauses."],"codes":["Topic 2"]},{"q":"You are talking to your French exchange partner about school. Say in French: (1) which subject you prefer and why; (2) ask what time school starts in France.","a":"e.g. Je préfère les maths parce que le prof est sympa. À quelle heure commence le collège en France ?","sol":"(1) needs je préfère + a subject with its article + parce que + a reason: Je préfère les maths parce que le prof est sympa. (2) needs a question with a wh-phrase: À quelle heure commence le collège en France ? (or: … est-ce que le collège commence en France ?).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Part 1 is an opinion with a reason; part 2 is a question about time.","Which verb gives 'prefer', and which question phrase asks 'at what time'?","One statement with a reason clause, then one question with a question mark."],"codes":["Topic 3"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Give the French for 'as … as' (equal comparison).","a":"aussi … que"},{"q":"Translate: 'on ne peut pas utiliser son portable en classe'.","a":"You cannot use your phone in class."},{"q":"What does the article become after trop de, beaucoup de and a negative verb?","a":"de (or d') with no article: trop de devoirs, pas de viande."},{"q":"Give the French reflexive verb for 'to get on well' (with someone).","a":"s'entendre bien (avec quelqu'un)"}]},"cards":[{"front":"sportif / sportive","back":"sporty (agrees with the person). Ma sœur est très sportive. (My sister is very sporty.)","code":"Topic 1","open":false},{"front":"plus / moins … que","back":"more / less … than. Il est plus grand que moi. (He is taller than me.)","code":"Topic 1","open":false},{"front":"s'entendre bien / mal avec","back":"to get on well / badly with. Je m'entends bien avec mon frère. (I get on well with my brother.)","code":"Topic 1","open":false},{"front":"compter sur quelqu'un","back":"to count on someone. Je peux compter sur mes amis. (I can count on my friends.)","code":"Topic 1","open":false},{"front":"se disputer","back":"to argue. Nous nous disputons parfois. (We sometimes argue.)","code":"Topic 1","open":false},{"front":"être en bonne santé","back":"to be in good health. Je suis en bonne santé. (I am in good health.)","code":"Topic 2","open":false},{"front":"faire du sport","back":"to do sport. Je fais du sport le samedi. (I do sport on Saturdays.)","code":"Topic 2","open":false},{"front":"se coucher / se lever","back":"to go to bed / to get up. Je me couche à dix heures. (I go to bed at ten.)","code":"Topic 2","open":false},{"front":"ne … jamais","back":"never. Je ne fume jamais. (I never smoke.)","code":"Topic 2","open":false},{"front":"il faut + infinitif","back":"you have to, it is necessary to. Il faut arriver à l'heure. (You have to arrive on time.)","code":"Topic 3","open":false},{"front":"on ne peut pas + infinitif","back":"you cannot. On ne peut pas manger en classe. (You cannot eat in class.)","code":"Topic 3","open":false},{"front":"un apprentissage","back":"an apprenticeship. Je vais chercher un apprentissage. (I am going to look for an apprenticeship.)","code":"Topic 3","open":false},{"front":"si + présent, aller + infinitif","back":"if + present, going to + verb. Si je réussis, je vais étudier les sciences. (If I pass, I am going to study science.)","code":"Topic 3","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulaire : identité et relations","items":["drôle - funny","travailleur / travailleuse - hard-working","gentil / gentille - kind","généreux / généreuse - generous","patient / patiente - patient","s'entendre bien / mal avec - to get on well / badly with","compter sur quelqu'un - to count on someone","le meilleur ami / la meilleure amie - best friend","se disputer - to argue","s'occuper de quelqu'un - to look after someone"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulaire : la vie saine","items":["mener une vie saine - to lead a healthy life","faire du sport - to do sport","le fast-food - fast food, junk food","les légumes / les fruits - vegetables / fruit","dormir assez - to sleep enough","se coucher / se lever - to go to bed / to get up","la santé - health","fumer - to smoke"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulaire : éducation et travail","items":["l'uniforme (m) - uniform","les devoirs (m) - homework","un apprentissage - an apprenticeship","réussir (un examen) - to pass (an exam)","le lycée - sixth form, upper secondary school","strict / stricte - strict","les sciences (f) - science","un petit boulot - a part-time job","gagner de l'argent - to earn money"]},{"kind":"grammar","title":"Grammaire : comparaisons, il faut et le futur proche","items":["plus / moins + adjectif + que + pronom tonique : Il est plus grand que moi. (He is taller than me.)","aussi + adjectif + que : Elle est aussi patiente que généreuse. (She is as patient as she is generous.)","de après une négation ou une quantité : Je ne mange pas de viande ; trop de devoirs. (I don't eat meat; too much homework.)","verbes pronominaux : je me couche, nous nous entendons bien. (I go to bed; we get on well.)","il faut + infinitif : Il faut étudier plus. (You have to study more.)","on ne peut pas + infinitif : On ne peut pas fumer ici. (You cannot smoke here.)","si + présent, aller + infinitif : Si je travaille dur, je vais réussir. (If I work hard, I am going to pass.)","depuis + présent : Elle s'occupe de moi depuis que je suis petite. (She has looked after me since I was small.)"]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-26T20:57:24.963Z","by":"claude-code-hand","models":["claude-fable-5-1","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"hand-written-2026-09-26-fr","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"All fifteen answer keys re-solve correctly and the French is accurate, the mark weights match the Paper 4 shapes, and the examples are clean; the only fixes needed are two hints that leak the 'de' rule, one misleading agreement hint, and lesson sections that never say how each topic surfaces in the speaking tasks."}}},"3.1.2":{"id":"AQA-8652","topic":"3.1.2","family":"language","lesson":{"why":"Theme 2 covers free-time activities, customs, festivals and celebrations, and celebrity culture — the topics where you describe what you and other people do, compare traditions, and give opinions about famous people. The structures that carry them are jouer à / jouer de and faire de for activities, on + verb for customs, the perfect tense for a festival you went to, aller + infinitive for what a celebrity is going to do, and the relative pronoun qui for saying who does what. Secure these and you can talk about your own leisure and your own view of fame rather than repeating set phrases.","idea":[{"code":"Topic 1","h":"Free-time activities","t":"This topic covers hobbies, sport, music, screens and going out, with how often you do them and what you think of them. Sports and games take jouer à (with the article contracting: jouer au foot, jouer aux jeux vidéo); musical instruments take jouer de (jouer de la guitare, jouer du piano); many activities take faire de (faire du vélo, faire de la natation). Frequency words sit close to the verb: Je joue souvent au tennis (I often play tennis); Je vais rarement au cinéma (I rarely go to the cinema); le samedi means every Saturday. Opinions use aimer, adorer, détester and préférer with the definite article: J'adore la musique, mais je déteste les jeux vidéo (I love music, but I hate video games). Add a reason with parce que every time you give an opinion: c'est relaxant, c'est trop cher. This topic supplies language for speaking as much as writing — the role-play, the reading-aloud task and the unprepared conversation — so practise saying these sentences aloud and answering a follow-up question about your own interests."},{"code":"Topic 2","h":"Customs, festivals and celebrations","t":"This topic covers festivals in France and the French-speaking world (le 14 juillet, Noël, le carnaval), family celebrations such as birthdays, and comparing them with traditions you know. A custom is described with on + a present-tense verb: On mange une galette en janvier (People eat a galette in January); Le soir, on regarde les feux d'artifice (In the evening people watch the fireworks). A festival you went to takes the perfect tense: Nous sommes allés à un festival et nous avons vu un défilé (We went to a festival and we saw a parade) — aller takes être and its past participle agrees, allés for nous. Comparison words link the two cultures: Dans mon pays, c'est différent, mais nous avons aussi des traditions à Noël (In my country it is different, but we also have traditions at Christmas). The same language is what you draw on when you are asked about a festival in conversation, so have a past-tense account of one celebration ready to say aloud."},{"code":"Topic 3","h":"Celebrity culture","t":"This topic covers famous people — singers, actors, sports stars, influencers — what they do and whether they are good role models. Jobs have masculine and feminine forms: un chanteur / une chanteuse, un acteur / une actrice. What a celebrity is going to do takes aller + infinitive: Cette chanteuse va sortir un nouvel album (This singer is going to release a new album) — note nouvel before a vowel. What they have done takes the perfect tense: Il a gagné un prix (He won a prize). Saying who does what uses the relative pronoun qui: J'admire les gens qui aident les autres (I admire people who help others). Social media vocabulary is central: les réseaux sociaux, un influenceur / une influenceuse, suivre quelqu'un (to follow someone). Give a balanced opinion: à mon avis, certaines célébrités sont de bons modèles, mais d'autres pensent seulement à l'argent. Expect to be asked your own view in conversation as well as in writing, and to justify it with a reason."}],"examples":[{"title":"Sports and instruments with jouer à and jouer de","setup":"Translate into French: 'I play football on Saturdays and I play the guitar in the evening.'","steps":["'Play football' is a sport, so it takes jouer à, and à + le contracts to au: je joue au foot.","'On Saturdays' — every Saturday — is le samedi, and 'in the evening' is le soir: both take the definite article and no plural.","'Play the guitar' is an instrument, so it takes jouer de, and de + la stays de la: je joue de la guitare.","Full sentence: Je joue au foot le samedi et je joue de la guitare le soir."],"cues":["Which preposition does jouer take with a sport, and does it contract with the article?","How do you say 'on Saturdays' and 'in the evening'?","Which preposition does jouer take with an instrument?","What is the complete sentence?"]},{"title":"Translating a text about a festival","setup":"Translate into English: 'En France, le 14 juillet, on regarde le défilé le matin et le soir il y a des feux d'artifice partout. Beaucoup de gens dansent dans la rue jusqu'à minuit.'","steps":["En France, le 14 juillet sets the scene — 'In France, on 14 July' — and on regarde le défilé le matin describes a custom with on: 'people watch the parade in the morning'.","le soir il y a des feux d'artifice partout is 'in the evening there are fireworks everywhere'.","Beaucoup de gens dansent dans la rue jusqu'à minuit is 'a lot of people dance in the street until midnight'.","Full translation: In France, on 14 July, people watch the parade in the morning and in the evening there are fireworks everywhere. A lot of people dance in the street until midnight."],"cues":["How is on translated when it describes what people generally do?","What does il y a introduce, and what does the final word add?","What do many people do, and until when?","Give the complete English translation."]},{"title":"A festival in the past with the perfect tense","setup":"Translate into French: 'Last year we went to a traditional festival and we saw a parade.'","steps":["'Last year' is l'année dernière.","'We went' uses aller, which takes être in the perfect tense, and the past participle agrees with nous: nous sommes allés.","'We saw' uses voir with avoir and the irregular participle vu: nous avons vu un défilé.","Full sentence: L'année dernière, nous sommes allés à une fête traditionnelle et nous avons vu un défilé."],"cues":["How do you say 'last year'?","Which auxiliary does aller take, and does the participle change for nous?","What is the past participle of voir, and which auxiliary does it take?","What is the complete sentence?"]},{"title":"What a celebrity is going to do","setup":"Translate into French: 'This singer (a woman) is going to release a new album and she is going to do a world tour.'","steps":["'This singer', a woman, is cette chanteuse.","'Is going to release' is aller + infinitive: va sortir; 'a new album' needs nouvel before the vowel of album: un nouvel album.","'She is going to do a world tour' is elle va faire une tournée mondiale.","Full sentence: Cette chanteuse va sortir un nouvel album et elle va faire une tournée mondiale."],"cues":["What are the feminine forms of 'this' and 'singer'?","Which structure gives 'is going to', and what form does nouveau take before album?","How do you say 'do a world tour'?","What is the complete sentence?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which preposition follows jouer for a sport, and which for an instrument?","a":"jouer à for a sport (jouer au foot); jouer de for an instrument (jouer de la guitare)."},{"q":"Which auxiliary does aller take in the perfect tense, and what happens to its past participle?","a":"être; the participle agrees with the subject: nous sommes allés, elle est allée."},{"q":"Which pronoun describes what people generally do at a festival?","a":"on + a present-tense verb: on danse, on mange."},{"q":"Which relative pronoun means 'who / which' as the subject of the next verb?","a":"qui: les gens qui aident les autres."}]},"room":{"facts":["jouer à + sport (jouer au foot, jouer aux jeux vidéo); jouer de + instrument (jouer de la guitare, jouer du piano); faire de + activity (faire du vélo).","Frequency: toujours, souvent, parfois, rarement, ne … jamais; le samedi / le week-end means every Saturday / every weekend.","on + present-tense verb describes a custom with no specific subject: on mange, on danse, on regarde.","The perfect tense uses avoir for most verbs (nous avons vu) and être for aller and other movement verbs, with participle agreement (nous sommes allés, elle est allée).","aller + infinitive gives a plan or near future (elle va sortir un album); qui introduces a relative clause whose subject it is (les gens qui aident)."],"questions":[{"q":"Translate into French: 'I love football.'","a":"J'adore le foot.","sol":"J'adore le foot. Verbs of liking take the definite article: le foot, never a bare noun.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Which verb expresses 'love' for an activity?","Does the noun after a verb of liking need an article?","Verb + article + noun."],"codes":["Topic 1"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'I usually watch series at the weekend.'","a":"D'habitude, je regarde des séries le week-end.","sol":"D'habitude, je regarde des séries le week-end. d'habitude gives 'usually'; des séries is the plural indefinite article; le week-end means at the weekend (every weekend).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Which phrase means 'usually'?","Which article goes before a plural noun like 'series'?","Frequency phrase, subject + verb + object, then the time phrase."],"codes":["Topic 1"]},{"q":"Translate into English: 'Je joue de la guitare tous les soirs.'","a":"I play the guitar every evening.","sol":"I play the guitar every evening. jouer de + instrument is 'to play' an instrument; tous les soirs is 'every evening'.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Which preposition follows jouer here, and what does that tell you about the object?","tous les soirs is a frequency phrase — what does it mean?","One complete English sentence."],"codes":["Topic 1"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'This actor is very famous.'","a":"Cet acteur est très célèbre.","sol":"Cet acteur est très célèbre. The demonstrative ce becomes cet before a masculine noun starting with a vowel; célèbre does not change in the masculine.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Which form of 'this' goes before a masculine noun starting with a vowel?","Is the adjective for famous the same for a man and a woman?","Demonstrative + noun + verb + très + adjective."],"codes":["Topic 3"]},{"q":"Translate into English: 'Le samedi, je fais souvent du vélo avec mes amis, mais je vais rarement au cinéma parce que c'est trop cher.'","a":"On Saturdays I often go cycling with my friends, but I rarely go to the cinema because it is too expensive.","sol":"On Saturdays I often go cycling with my friends, but I rarely go to the cinema because it is too expensive. le samedi = every Saturday; faire du vélo = to go cycling; souvent and rarement are frequency adverbs placed after the verb.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Two frequency adverbs sit after the verbs — find both.","faire du vélo is an activity phrase — what does it mean?","One complete English sentence with a contrast and a reason."],"codes":["Topic 1"]},{"q":"Read this text and answer in English: 'Le week-end, je retrouve mes amis pour regarder un film ou jouer aux jeux vidéo en ligne. Je sors rarement pendant la semaine parce que j'ai beaucoup de devoirs.' What does the writer usually do at weekends, and how often do they go out during the week?","a":"They meet friends to watch a film or play video games online; they rarely go out during the week (because of homework).","sol":"je retrouve mes amis pour regarder un film ou jouer aux jeux vidéo en ligne — I meet my friends to watch a film or play video games online; je sors rarement pendant la semaine — I rarely go out during the week; parce que j'ai beaucoup de devoirs — because I have a lot of homework.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Find the two weekend activities after pour in the first sentence.","rarement is a frequency adverb — what does it mean?","Two short English answers, one per sentence."],"codes":["Topic 1"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'In my country we don't celebrate this festival, but we have similar traditions at Christmas.'","a":"Dans mon pays, nous ne célébrons pas cette fête, mais nous avons des traditions similaires à Noël.","sol":"Dans mon pays, nous ne célébrons pas cette fête, mais nous avons des traditions similaires à Noël. ne … pas wraps the verb célébrons; cette agrees with the feminine fête; similaires agrees with the plural traditions; à Noël for at Christmas.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Where do the two parts of the negative go around the verb?","Check the agreement of 'this' and 'similar' with their nouns.","Place phrase, then a negative clause, then a contrast clause with the time phrase last."],"codes":["Topic 2"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'Many young people follow influencers on social media because they find their videos funny.'","a":"Beaucoup de jeunes suivent des influenceurs sur les réseaux sociaux parce qu'ils trouvent leurs vidéos drôles.","sol":"Beaucoup de jeunes suivent des influenceurs sur les réseaux sociaux parce qu'ils trouvent leurs vidéos drôles. beaucoup de takes no article; suivent is the ils form of suivre; leurs agrees with the plural vidéos; parce que shortens to parce qu' before ils.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Which verb means 'to follow', and what is its ils form?","Which possessive agrees with a plural noun owned by several people?","Subject + verb + object + place phrase, then a reason clause."],"codes":["Topic 3"]},{"q":"Read this text and answer in English: 'Certains acteurs célèbres utilisent leur argent pour aider les autres, mais d'autres pensent seulement à eux-mêmes, et je trouve ça triste.' What contrast does the writer draw, and what is their opinion of the second group?","a":"Some famous actors use their money to help others, while others only think of themselves; the writer finds that sad.","sol":"certains acteurs célèbres utilisent leur argent pour aider les autres — some famous actors use their money to help others; d'autres pensent seulement à eux-mêmes — others think only of themselves; je trouve ça triste — I find that sad.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["certains … d'autres sets up two groups — find both.","The opinion comes after je trouve.","Give the contrast and the writer's opinion, in English."],"codes":["Topic 3"]},{"q":"Write about 50 words in French about your free time, covering these five points: your favourite hobby; when you do it; who you do it with; a hobby you don't like; what you are going to do this weekend.","a":"e.g. Mon passe-temps préféré, c'est le foot. Je joue au foot le samedi matin avec mes copains au parc près de chez moi. Je n'aime pas la natation parce que l'eau est trop froide. Ce week-end, je vais aller au cinéma avec ma sœur pour voir un film d'action.","sol":"Mon passe-temps préféré, c'est le foot. Je joue au foot le samedi matin avec mes copains au parc près de chez moi. Je n'aime pas la natation parce que l'eau est trop froide. Ce week-end, je vais aller au cinéma avec ma sœur pour voir un film d'action. (48 words, covering the five points in four sentences: favourite hobby; when and with whom together; a disliked hobby with a reason; a plan with aller + infinitive.)","m":10,"d":2,"hints":["Which structures give a like, a dislike and a plan?","Give one sentence per point, in the order listed.","About 50 words in five short sentences."],"codes":["Topic 1"]},{"q":"You are talking to a French friend about celebrations. Say in French: (1) what you did last Christmas; (2) ask what your friend does on 14 July.","a":"e.g. Noël dernier, j'ai mangé avec ma famille et j'ai reçu des cadeaux. Qu'est-ce que tu fais le 14 juillet ?","sol":"(1) needs a past time marker (Noël dernier / l'année dernière) and the perfect tense: j'ai mangé, j'ai reçu, je suis allé(e) chez mes grands-parents. (2) needs a question to a friend as tu: Qu'est-ce que tu fais le 14 juillet ? (or: Tu fais quoi le 14 juillet ?).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Part 1 is in the past; part 2 is a question to a friend.","Which past tense reports what you did, and which question phrase opens a question to tu?","One past-tense statement, then one question with a question mark."],"codes":["Topic 2"]},{"q":"Write a paragraph of about 90 words in French comparing a festival in a French-speaking country with one in your own country, covering: (1) how the French festival is celebrated; (2) one similarity or difference with a festival you know; (3) your opinion of it.","a":"e.g. En France, le 14 juillet, on fête la fête nationale. Le matin, il y a un grand défilé à Paris et, le soir, on regarde les feux d'artifice en famille. Beaucoup de gens dansent dans la rue jusqu'à minuit. Dans mon pays, nous n'avons pas de fête nationale comme ça, mais nous avons des feux d'artifice en novembre. À mon avis, le 14 juillet est une fête formidable parce que tout le monde est ensemble, et l'année prochaine, je vais aller à Paris pour la voir.","sol":"En France, le 14 juillet, on fête la fête nationale. Le matin, il y a un grand défilé à Paris et, le soir, on regarde les feux d'artifice en famille. Beaucoup de gens dansent dans la rue jusqu'à minuit. Dans mon pays, nous n'avons pas de fête nationale comme ça, mais nous avons des feux d'artifice en novembre. À mon avis, le 14 juillet est une fête formidable parce que tout le monde est ensemble, et l'année prochaine, je vais aller à Paris pour la voir. (86 words: on + verb for the custom, a comparison with the writer's own country, an opinion with a reason, and a future plan with aller + infinitive.)","m":15,"d":3,"hints":["Which structure describes how a custom is carried out without naming who does it?","Which words compare one tradition with another, and which phrase gives an opinion?","Three short sections in the order of the bullets, about 90 words in all."],"codes":["Topic 2"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'Even if I don't often follow celebrity news, I admire people who use their fame to help others.'","a":"Même si je ne suis pas souvent les infos sur les célébrités, j'admire les gens qui utilisent leur célébrité pour aider les autres.","sol":"Même si je ne suis pas souvent les infos sur les célébrités, j'admire les gens qui utilisent leur célébrité pour aider les autres. je suis here is from suivre (to follow), not être; qui introduces the relative clause whose subject is les gens; pour + infinitive gives the purpose.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["'Follow' is an irregular verb whose je form looks like another very common verb — which?","Which relative pronoun links 'people' to what they do?","Concession clause, main clause, then a relative clause ending with a purpose phrase."],"codes":["Topic 3"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'Last year we went to a traditional festival in a small village, where they organised a parade and everyone danced in the street until midnight.'","a":"L'année dernière, nous sommes allés à une fête traditionnelle dans un petit village, où on a organisé un défilé et tout le monde a dansé dans la rue jusqu'à minuit.","sol":"L'année dernière, nous sommes allés à une fête traditionnelle dans un petit village, où on a organisé un défilé et tout le monde a dansé dans la rue jusqu'à minuit. aller takes être with agreement (allés); où introduces the relative clause about the place; 'they organised' with no specific subject is on a organisé; tout le monde takes a singular verb (a dansé).","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Which auxiliary does aller take, and does its participle agree?","Which relative word means 'where', and which pronoun renders an unspecified 'they'?","Time phrase, main clause with the place, then a relative clause with two past-tense events."],"codes":["Topic 2"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'This actor has won a prize for his new film, and many young people think he is a good role model.'","a":"Cet acteur a gagné un prix pour son nouveau film, et beaucoup de jeunes pensent que c'est un bon modèle.","sol":"Cet acteur a gagné un prix pour son nouveau film, et beaucoup de jeunes pensent que c'est un bon modèle. a gagné is the perfect tense with avoir; nouveau stays nouveau before the consonant of film; before un + noun French uses c'est, not il est.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Which tense is 'has won', and which auxiliary does gagner take?","Which form of 'this' goes before acteur, and which pronoun does French use before un + noun?","Subject + perfect-tense verb + object + purpose phrase, then a second clause with subject + verb + a that-clause."],"codes":["Topic 3"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Give the French for 'to play the piano'.","a":"jouer du piano"},{"q":"Translate: 'nous sommes allés à un festival'.","a":"we went to a festival"},{"q":"Which pronoun + present tense describes what people generally do at a festival?","a":"on + present tense (on danse, on mange)."},{"q":"Give the French for 'she is going to release an album'.","a":"elle va sortir un album"}]},"cards":[{"front":"jouer au foot / aux jeux vidéo","back":"to play football / video games (jouer à + sport or game). Je joue au foot le samedi.","code":"Topic 1","open":false},{"front":"jouer de la guitare / du piano","back":"to play the guitar / the piano (jouer de + instrument). Je joue du piano le soir.","code":"Topic 1","open":false},{"front":"faire du vélo","back":"to go cycling. Je fais du vélo avec mes amis.","code":"Topic 1","open":false},{"front":"souvent / rarement","back":"often / rarely. Je vais rarement au cinéma.","code":"Topic 1","open":false},{"front":"retrouver ses amis","back":"to meet up with one's friends. Je retrouve mes amis le week-end.","code":"Topic 1","open":false},{"front":"un défilé","back":"a parade. On regarde le défilé le matin.","code":"Topic 2","open":false},{"front":"les feux d'artifice","back":"fireworks. Le soir, il y a des feux d'artifice.","code":"Topic 2","open":false},{"front":"fêter / célébrer","back":"to celebrate. Nous fêtons Noël en famille.","code":"Topic 2","open":false},{"front":"nous sommes allés","back":"we went (aller takes être; the participle agrees). Nous sommes allés à une fête.","code":"Topic 2","open":false},{"front":"un chanteur / une chanteuse","back":"a singer (m / f). Cette chanteuse est très célèbre.","code":"Topic 3","open":false},{"front":"les réseaux sociaux","back":"social media. Je suis des influenceurs sur les réseaux sociaux.","code":"Topic 3","open":false},{"front":"un modèle","back":"a role model. C'est un bon modèle pour les jeunes.","code":"Topic 3","open":false},{"front":"suivre (je suis, ils suivent)","back":"to follow. Beaucoup de jeunes suivent des influenceurs.","code":"Topic 3","open":false},{"front":"les gens qui …","back":"people who … (qui = subject of the next verb). J'admire les gens qui aident les autres.","code":"Topic 3","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulaire : les loisirs","items":["jouer aux jeux vidéo - to play video games","regarder un film / une série - to watch a film / a series","lire un roman - to read a novel","retrouver ses amis - to meet up with friends","faire du sport - to do sport","écouter de la musique - to listen to music","en ligne - online","le week-end - the weekend, at the weekend","le passe-temps - hobby","la natation - swimming"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulaire : fêtes et célébrations","items":["un défilé - a parade","les feux d'artifice - fireworks","une fête - a festival, a celebration","fêter / célébrer - to celebrate","Noël - Christmas","le 14 juillet - 14 July (French national day)","un cadeau - a present","la tradition - tradition","pareil / similaire - similar","différent / différente - different"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulaire : la célébrité et les réseaux sociaux","items":["une célébrité - a celebrity","célèbre - famous","un chanteur / une chanteuse - a singer","un acteur / une actrice - an actor / actress","les réseaux sociaux - social media","un influenceur / une influenceuse - an influencer","suivre - to follow","un prix - a prize, an award","un modèle - a role model","une tournée - a tour"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulaire : sport et musique","items":["le foot - football","le tennis - tennis","la guitare - guitar","le piano - piano","une équipe - a team","un match - a match","gagner - to win","une chanson - a song","un concert - a concert","un groupe - a band"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulaire : fêtes de famille","items":["un anniversaire - a birthday","un gâteau - a cake","un repas - a meal","les grands-parents - grandparents","inviter - to invite","danser - to dance","chanter - to sing","offrir - to give (a present)","recevoir - to receive","le mariage - wedding"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulaire : opinions sur la célébrité","items":["à mon avis - in my opinion","admirer - to admire","la célébrité - fame; a celebrity","riche - rich","l'argent (m) - money","aider les autres - to help others","penser seulement à soi - to think only of oneself","triste - sad","un exemple - an example","la vie privée - private life"]},{"kind":"grammar","title":"Grammaire : jouer à / de, on, le passé composé et le futur proche","items":["jouer à + sport (au, aux) ; jouer de + instrument (du, de la) : Je joue au tennis et je joue de la guitare. (I play tennis and I play the guitar.)","on + présent pour une coutume : On mange une galette en janvier. (People eat a galette in January.)","passé composé avec avoir : Nous avons vu un défilé. (We saw a parade.)","passé composé avec être + accord : Nous sommes allés à Paris ; elle est allée au festival. (We went to Paris; she went to the festival.)","aller + infinitif : Elle va sortir un album. (She is going to release an album.)","qui + verbe (pronom relatif sujet) : J'admire les gens qui aident les autres. (I admire people who help others.)","ce / cet / cette / ces : cet acteur, cette chanteuse. (this actor, this singer)","nouveau / nouvel / nouvelle : un nouveau film, un nouvel album, une nouvelle chanson. (a new film, a new album, a new song)"]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-26T20:57:24.964Z","by":"claude-code-hand","models":["claude-fable-5-1","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"hand-written-2026-09-26-fr","judge":{"score":0.82,"notes":"Every answer key re-solved correctly (translations, comprehension and both writing models are accurate French and match their solutions, and the mark allocations fit the Foundation/Higher shapes), so the only fixes needed are the four-versus-five-sentence clash in the 50-word question's third hint, the mislabelled 'purpose phrase' in the last question, and trimming the task-type name-checks from the lesson sections."}}},"3.1.3":{"id":"AQA-8652","topic":"3.1.3","family":"language","lesson":{"why":"Theme 3 covers travel and tourism, media and technology, and the environment and where people live — the topics where you describe places, compare them, weigh an advantage against a disadvantage and say what should be done. They rely on two past tenses working together (the imperfect to set a scene, the perfect tense to report what happened), on pour + infinitive to say what you use something for, on il faut and on doit for what has to be done, and on comparisons between town and country. Secure these and you can describe a real trip, a real habit and a real local problem in your own words.","idea":[{"code":"1","h":"Travel and tourism, including places of interest","t":"This topic covers describing a place you have visited or would like to visit, holiday activities, and comparing destinations. The imperfect sets the scene for a past holiday: Quand j'étais en vacances, il faisait très chaud et le village était très calme (When I was on holiday, the weather was very hot and the village was very quiet) — étais/était, faisait and il y avait are the forms you need most. The perfect tense reports what you actually did: Un jour, nous avons visité un vieux château et nous avons pris beaucoup de photos (One day we visited an old castle and took a lot of photos); verbs of movement take être, with agreement: je suis allé(e), nous sommes partis. Comparing places uses plus / moins + adjective + que and aussi … que: Cette ville est plus calme que mon village, mais elle n'est pas aussi intéressante que Paris. Be ready to describe a real place you know in the imperfect and then say one thing you did there in the perfect tense."},{"code":"2","h":"Media and technology","t":"This topic covers how you use your phone, the internet and social media, and opinions on their advantages and disadvantages. Describe use with utiliser … pour + infinitive: J'utilise mon portable pour prendre des photos et pour chercher des informations (I use my phone to take photos and to look for information). on peut + infinitive gives what is possible in general: On peut regarder des vidéos partout (You can watch videos everywhere). Balanced opinions set an advantage against a disadvantage with mais or pourtant: Les réseaux sociaux permettent de rester en contact avec ses amis, mais on perd beaucoup de temps (Social media lets you stay in touch with your friends, but you waste a lot of time). Time phrases quantify the habit: trois heures par jour, tous les soirs. Have one balanced sentence ready that weighs an advantage against a disadvantage, with a reason for your own view."},{"code":"3","h":"The environment and where people live","t":"This topic covers environmental problems and solutions, and describing where you live. il faut and on doit + infinitive express what needs to be done: Il faut recycler plus et utiliser moins de plastique (We need to recycle more and use less plastic). Problems are stated with il y a trop de: Il y a trop de déchets dans les rues (There is too much rubbish in the streets). Describing your area uses j'habite + place and il y a: J'habite dans un petit appartement au centre-ville ; il y a beaucoup de magasins mais il n'y a pas de parc. Comparisons contrast town and country: La campagne est plus calme que la ville, mais la ville a plus de transports en commun (The countryside is quieter than the city, but the city has more public transport). Be ready to describe a town or landscape and then say what should be done about a problem in it."}],"examples":[{"title":"Imperfect for the scene, perfect tense for the event","setup":"Translate into French: 'The beach was beautiful and it was very hot, so on the last day we went to a great restaurant.'","steps":["'The beach was beautiful' and 'it was very hot' describe the ongoing background, so they take the imperfect: La plage était magnifique ; il faisait très chaud.","'So' links the scene to what happened as a result: donc; 'on the last day' is the time phrase le dernier jour.","'We went to a great restaurant' is a single completed event, so it takes the perfect tense, with être for aller and agreement for nous: nous sommes allés dans un très bon restaurant.","Full sentence: La plage était magnifique et il faisait très chaud, donc le dernier jour, nous sommes allés dans un très bon restaurant."],"cues":["Which two details describe the ongoing background rather than an event, and which tense do they take?","Which word links the scene to the result, and how is 'on the last day' expressed?","Which tense reports a single completed event, and which auxiliary does aller take?","How do the parts combine into the full sentence?"]},{"title":"Translating a text about using technology","setup":"Translate into English: 'J'utilise mon portable surtout pour parler avec mes amis et pour faire mes devoirs en ligne, mais parfois je passe trop de temps sur les réseaux sociaux.'","steps":["J'utilise mon portable surtout pour … is 'I use my phone mainly to …'.","parler avec mes amis et pour faire mes devoirs en ligne gives the two uses: 'talk to my friends and to do my homework online'.","mais parfois je passe trop de temps sur les réseaux sociaux is 'but sometimes I spend too much time on social media'.","Full translation: I use my phone mainly to talk to my friends and to do my homework online, but sometimes I spend too much time on social media."],"cues":["What does surtout mean here?","What are the two uses named after pour?","What does the clause after mais say?","Give the complete English translation."]},{"title":"Saying what has to be done about a problem","setup":"Translate into French: 'You have to recycle more and use less plastic, because there is too much rubbish in the streets.'","steps":["'You have to' in the general sense is il faut + infinitive: il faut recycler plus.","'Use less plastic' continues with a second infinitive: et utiliser moins de plastique — moins de takes no article.","'There is too much rubbish' is il y a trop de déchets, again with de and no article, and 'in the streets' is dans les rues.","Full sentence: Il faut recycler plus et utiliser moins de plastique, parce qu'il y a trop de déchets dans les rues."],"cues":["Which impersonal phrase gives 'you have to', and what verb form follows it?","How is 'less plastic' expressed — with an article or without?","How do you say 'there is too much rubbish'?","What is the complete sentence?"]},{"title":"Comparing town and country","setup":"Translate into French: 'I am going to move to the countryside next year, because it is quieter than the city and there is less traffic.'","steps":["'I am going to move' is aller + infinitive: je vais déménager.","'To the countryside next year' follows: à la campagne l'année prochaine.","'Because it is quieter than the city' is a comparison after parce que: parce que c'est plus calme que la ville.","'And there is less traffic' adds a second reason: et il y a moins de circulation. Full sentence: Je vais déménager à la campagne l'année prochaine, parce que c'est plus calme que la ville et il y a moins de circulation."],"cues":["Which structure expresses a plan for the near future?","How do you say 'to the countryside' and 'next year'?","Which comparison structure means 'quieter than', and what introduces the reason?","How do you say 'there is less traffic', and how does the whole sentence fit together?"]},{"title":"Answering a comprehension question on a holiday text","setup":"Read: 'Nous avons passé une semaine à Lyon. L'hôtel était près de la gare, mais il faisait trop chaud pour visiter la ville l'après-midi.' Answer in English: where was the hotel, and why did they not visit the city in the afternoon?","steps":["Find the sentence about the hotel: L'hôtel était près de la gare — the hotel was near the station.","Find the reason clause after mais: il faisait trop chaud — it was too hot.","Link the reason to what it prevented: pour visiter la ville l'après-midi — to visit the city in the afternoon.","Answer: the hotel was near the station; they did not visit the city in the afternoon because it was too hot."],"cues":["Which sentence tells you where the hotel was, and what does it say?","What comes after mais, and what does it mean?","What does the infinitive phrase after chaud tell you was prevented?","What are the two answers in English?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which tense sets the scene of a past holiday (weather, what a place was like), and which reports what you did?","a":"Imperfect for the scene (il faisait, c'était); perfect tense for the events (nous avons visité)."},{"q":"Give the French for 'I use it to' + verb.","a":"Je l'utilise pour + infinitive."},{"q":"Give two French ways of saying 'we have to' + verb, one impersonal.","a":"il faut + infinitive; on doit / nous devons + infinitive."},{"q":"Translate: 'la campagne est plus calme que la ville'.","a":"The countryside is quieter than the city."}]},"room":{"facts":["Imperfect sets the scene (c'était, il faisait, il y avait); the perfect tense reports completed events (nous avons visité, je suis allé).","utiliser + object + pour + infinitive says what something is used for: J'utilise mon portable pour prendre des photos.","il faut / on doit + infinitive express what needs to be done, without naming who must do it.","trop de, moins de, plus de and beaucoup de take no article: trop de déchets, moins de plastique.","plus / moins + adjective + que and aussi + adjective + que compare places exactly as they compare people."],"questions":[{"q":"Translate into French: 'The hotel was very comfortable.'","a":"L'hôtel était très confortable.","sol":"L'hôtel était très confortable. The sentence describes what the hotel was like, so the imperfect était is the right choice.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Is this a description of an ongoing state, or a single completed action?","Which past tense fits a description?","Subject, past-tense verb, then très + adjective."],"codes":["1"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'I use my laptop to do my homework.'","a":"J'utilise mon ordinateur portable pour faire mes devoirs.","sol":"J'utilise mon ordinateur portable pour faire mes devoirs. pour + infinitive expresses the purpose; mon ordinateur portable (or mon ordi) is the laptop.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Which small word links 'use' to the purpose that follows?","Is the verb after that word conjugated, or an infinitive?","Present-tense verb, object, then the purpose phrase with an infinitive."],"codes":["2"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'We have to protect the environment.'","a":"Il faut protéger l'environnement. (or: Nous devons / On doit protéger l'environnement.)","sol":"Il faut protéger l'environnement. il faut + infinitive expresses a general need without naming who must act; nous devons or on doit + infinitive are equally correct.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Which impersonal phrase expresses a general need?","What form does the verb take after it?","Impersonal phrase, infinitive, then the object with its article."],"codes":["3"]},{"q":"Translate into English: 'L'année dernière, nous avons visité un petit village près de la côte.'","a":"Last year we visited a small village near the coast.","sol":"Last year we visited a small village near the coast. nous avons visité is the perfect tense of visiter; près de is 'near'.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["What time phrase opens the sentence, and what does it tell you about the tense?","près de is a place phrase — what does it mean?","One English sentence: time phrase, 'we' + past verb, then the place."],"codes":["1"]},{"q":"Read this text and answer in English: 'Quand j'étais petit, ma famille et moi, nous faisions du camping tous les étés, mais l'année dernière, pour la première fois, nous sommes restés dans un hôtel de luxe.' What did the writer's family use to do, and what was different last year?","a":"They used to go camping every summer; last year, for the first time, they stayed in a luxury hotel.","sol":"nous faisions du camping tous les étés — we used to go camping every summer (imperfect for a repeated habit); l'année dernière, pour la première fois, nous sommes restés dans un hôtel de luxe — last year, for the first time, we stayed in a luxury hotel (perfect tense for the one-off event).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Look at the tense of the first verb — what does it say about how often the action happened?","Look for the connector that turns the sentence round, and the tense of the verb after it.","Two English answers: the old habit, then the one-off change."],"codes":["1"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'Social media lets you stay in touch with your friends, but it can also be a waste of time.'","a":"Les réseaux sociaux permettent de rester en contact avec ses amis, mais ça peut aussi être une perte de temps.","sol":"Les réseaux sociaux permettent de rester en contact avec ses amis, mais ça peut aussi être une perte de temps. réseaux sociaux is plural, so permettent; permettre de + infinitive; peut + infinitive for 'can be'; une perte de temps is the set phrase.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Is the subject singular or plural in French, and what does that do to the verb 'lets'?","'Can be' needs a modal verb plus an infinitive — which?","One sentence with two clauses joined by mais."],"codes":["2"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'There is a lot of rubbish in the street, and that is bad for the environment.'","a":"Il y a beaucoup de déchets dans la rue, et c'est mauvais pour l'environnement.","sol":"Il y a beaucoup de déchets dans la rue, et c'est mauvais pour l'environnement. beaucoup de takes no article; c'est refers back to the whole idea; mauvais pour + noun.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Which phrase gives 'there is', and what follows an expression of quantity?","Which short phrase refers back to the whole idea just stated?","One sentence with two clauses joined by et."],"codes":["3"]},{"q":"Read this text and answer in English: 'J'habite dans un petit appartement au centre-ville. J'aime ça parce que tout est très près, mais parfois la ville est trop bruyante et je préfère le calme de la campagne.' What does the writer like about their home, and what do they sometimes prefer?","a":"They like that everything is very close; they sometimes prefer the calm of the countryside (because the city is too noisy).","sol":"J'aime ça parce que tout est très près — I like it because everything is very close; parfois la ville est trop bruyante et je préfère le calme de la campagne — sometimes the city is too noisy and I prefer the calm of the countryside.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Find the reason clause after parce que for the first answer.","The second answer comes after mais — find what the writer prefers.","Two English answers: the reason for liking it, then what is sometimes preferred."],"codes":["3"]},{"q":"Write about 50 words in French about your town, covering these five points: where you live; what there is in your town; what you like about it; a problem; what you are going to do to help the environment.","a":"About 50 words, one sentence per point: where (j'habite à …), what there is (il y a …), what you like with a reason, a problem (il y a trop de …), a plan (je vais …).","sol":"J'habite à Leeds, une grande ville dans le nord de l'Angleterre. Il y a beaucoup de magasins et un grand parc. J'aime ma ville parce que mes amis habitent près de chez moi. Mais il y a trop de circulation. Ce week-end, je vais recycler le papier et aller au collège à vélo. (53 words, one point per sentence, with il y a, an opinion with a reason, trop de for the problem and aller + infinitive for the plan.)","m":10,"d":2,"hints":["Which structures say what there is, what you like, and what you are going to do?","Give one sentence per point, in the order listed.","About 50 words in five short sentences."],"codes":["3"]},{"q":"Translate into French (the speaker is a boy): 'Last summer I went to Spain with my family and the weather was great.'","a":"L'été dernier, je suis allé en Espagne avec ma famille et il faisait très beau.","sol":"L'été dernier, je suis allé en Espagne avec ma famille et il faisait très beau. aller takes être in the perfect tense (je suis allé); en before a feminine country (en Espagne); the weather is a description, so the imperfect il faisait beau.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Which auxiliary does aller take in the perfect tense?","Which preposition goes before a feminine country, and which tense describes the weather as a background?","Time phrase, perfect-tense clause, et, then the weather clause."],"codes":["1"]},{"q":"At a tourist office in France. Say in French: (1) that you would like to visit the castle; (2) ask what time it opens.","a":"e.g. Je voudrais visiter le château. Il ouvre à quelle heure ?","sol":"(1) je voudrais + infinitive: Je voudrais visiter le château. (2) a question about time: Il ouvre à quelle heure ? (or: À quelle heure est-ce qu'il ouvre ?).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Which polite form of vouloir means 'I would like'?","Which question phrase asks 'at what time'?","One statement, then one question with a question mark."],"codes":["1"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'Even if my old phone was slower, I preferred it, because I spent less time on social media.'","a":"Même si mon ancien portable était plus lent, je le préférais, parce que je passais moins de temps sur les réseaux sociaux.","sol":"Même si mon ancien portable était plus lent, je le préférais, parce que je passais moins de temps sur les réseaux sociaux. All three verbs describe ongoing past states or habits, so they take the imperfect (était, préférais, passais); le is the direct object pronoun before the verb; moins de takes no article.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Are the three verbs one-off completed actions, or ongoing states and habits? Which tense fits that?","'Preferred it' needs a direct object pronoun before the verb — which one?","Concession clause, main clause with the pronoun, then a reason clause."],"codes":["2"]},{"q":"Write a paragraph of about 90 words in French about a place you have visited, covering: your general impression of it; one specific thing you did there; whether you would like to return and why.","a":"About 90 words: an imperfect description (e.g. la ville était magnifique, il faisait chaud), one perfect-tense event (nous avons visité …), and je voudrais retourner with a reason.","sol":"L'été dernier, je suis allé à Nice avec ma famille. La ville était magnifique, avec ses vieilles rues étroites, et il faisait très chaud tous les jours, donc nous allions à la plage chaque matin. Un jour, nous avons visité le vieux port et nous avons mangé dans un petit restaurant près de la mer ; c'était délicieux. Je voudrais retourner à Nice l'année prochaine parce que les gens étaient très sympas et il y a beaucoup de choses à faire, surtout pour les jeunes comme moi. (87 words: imperfect for the description, perfect tense for the specific event, je voudrais + infinitive for the wish, with reasons.)","m":15,"d":3,"hints":["Which two past tenses do you need — one for what the place was like, one for what you did there?","Separate the scene (what the place was like) from the one thing that happened, and give each its own kind of past verb.","About 90 words in three short parts: the place, the one thing you did, then whether you would return and why."],"codes":["1"]},{"q":"Write about 150 words in French on technology and the environment where you live, covering: (1) how you and your family use technology day to day, with one advantage and one disadvantage; (2) an environmental problem in your area and what should be done about it.","a":"About 150 words: how the family uses technology (utiliser … pour), one advantage and one disadvantage, then a local environmental problem (il y a trop de …) with what should be done (il faut …) and an opinion.","sol":"Dans ma famille, on utilise beaucoup la technologie : mes parents travaillent à la maison avec leur ordinateur, et moi, j'utilise mon portable pour étudier et pour parler avec mes amis. Un avantage, c'est qu'on peut trouver des informations et rester en contact avec la famille qui habite loin. Pourtant, il y a aussi un inconvénient : je passe parfois trois heures par jour sur les réseaux sociaux, et après, je suis fatigué et je ne fais pas mes devoirs. L'année dernière, j'ai passé trop de temps sur mon portable et mes notes étaient mauvaises, donc maintenant je l'utilise moins souvent. Là où j'habite, le problème principal, ce sont les déchets. Il y a trop de plastique dans les rues et dans la rivière, parce que beaucoup de gens ne recyclent pas. Pour changer ça, il faut mettre plus de poubelles au centre-ville et organiser des journées de nettoyage. Je pense que si tout le monde fait un petit effort, notre ville sera plus propre. (165 words: pour + infinitive for uses, an advantage and a disadvantage with pourtant, a perfect-tense and imperfect example for last year, il faut for the solution, the future sera, and a justified opinion.)","m":25,"d":3,"hints":["Which structures say what you use technology for, and what should be done about a problem?","The technology section needs a genuine balance between the advantage and the disadvantage, not just two separate opinions.","About 150 words in two parts: technology (balanced), then the problem and its solution, with an opinion."],"codes":["2","3"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Translate: 'il faisait très chaud'.","a":"it was very hot (the weather)"},{"q":"Give the French for 'I use my phone to take photos'.","a":"J'utilise mon portable pour prendre des photos."},{"q":"Give the French for 'there is too much rubbish'.","a":"il y a trop de déchets"},{"q":"Which auxiliary does aller take in the perfect tense, and what is 'I went' for a girl?","a":"être; je suis allée."}]},"cards":[{"front":"il faisait chaud","back":"it was hot (imperfect, for the scene). Il faisait chaud tous les jours.","code":"1","open":false},{"front":"prendre des photos","back":"to take photos. Nous avons pris beaucoup de photos.","code":"1","open":false},{"front":"je suis allé / allée","back":"I went (aller with être; agree with the subject). Je suis allée à Nice.","code":"1","open":false},{"front":"le château","back":"the castle. Nous avons visité un vieux château.","code":"1","open":false},{"front":"utiliser … pour + infinitif","back":"to use … to. J'utilise mon portable pour parler avec mes amis.","code":"2","open":false},{"front":"les réseaux sociaux","back":"social media. Je passe trop de temps sur les réseaux sociaux.","code":"2","open":false},{"front":"rester en contact avec","back":"to stay in touch with. Je reste en contact avec mes cousins.","code":"2","open":false},{"front":"une perte de temps","back":"a waste of time. Ça peut être une perte de temps.","code":"2","open":false},{"front":"il faut + infinitif","back":"we have to, it is necessary to. Il faut recycler plus.","code":"3","open":false},{"front":"les déchets","back":"rubbish, waste. Il y a trop de déchets dans les rues.","code":"3","open":false},{"front":"la campagne / la ville","back":"the countryside / the city. La campagne est plus calme que la ville.","code":"3","open":false},{"front":"la circulation","back":"traffic. Il y a moins de circulation à la campagne.","code":"3","open":false},{"front":"Give one environmental problem and one solution, in French","back":"il y a trop de déchets — il faut recycler plus; il y a trop de circulation — on doit prendre le bus; il y a trop de plastique — il faut utiliser moins de plastique","code":"3","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulaire : voyages et tourisme","items":["en vacances - on holiday","un château - a castle","la côte - the coast","la plage - the beach","prendre des photos - to take photos","rester (dans un hôtel) - to stay (in a hotel)","pour la première fois - for the first time","confortable - comfortable","magnifique - beautiful, magnificent","visiter - to visit (a place)"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulaire : hébergement et transport","items":["l'hôtel (m) - hotel","le vol - flight","le train - train","la gare - station","l'aéroport (m) - airport","louer - to rent, hire","une excursion - a trip, excursion","l'office de tourisme (m) - tourist office"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulaire : médias et technologie","items":["le portable - mobile phone","l'ordinateur portable (m) - laptop","les réseaux sociaux - social media","rester en contact - to stay in touch","une perte de temps - a waste of time","l'écran (m) - the screen","en ligne - online","un avantage / un inconvénient - an advantage / a disadvantage","chercher des informations - to look for information"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulaire : avantages et inconvénients de la technologie","items":["pratique - practical, handy","rapide - fast","gratuit / gratuite - free","dangereux / dangereuse - dangerous","la vie privée - privacy, private life","passer du temps - to spend time","trop de temps - too much time","fatigué / fatiguée - tired","apprendre - to learn","les notes (f) - marks"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulaire : l'environnement","items":["recycler / le recyclage - to recycle / recycling","l'environnement (m) - the environment","la pollution - pollution","les déchets (m) - rubbish, waste","le plastique - plastic","la circulation - traffic","les transports en commun - public transport","la campagne / la ville - the countryside / the city","bruyant / bruyante - noisy","calme - quiet, calm","le centre-ville - the town centre"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulaire : là où j'habite","items":["un appartement - a flat","une maison - a house","le quartier - the neighbourhood","le nord / le sud - the north / the south","près de - near","loin de - far from","habiter - to live","déménager - to move house","la rivière - the river","la poubelle - the bin"]},{"kind":"grammar","title":"Grammaire : l'imparfait, le passé composé et il faut","items":["l'imparfait pour la description et les habitudes : Le village était très calme et il faisait beau tous les jours. (The village was very quiet and it was sunny every day.)","le passé composé pour les actions terminées : Un jour, nous avons visité un château et nous avons pris des photos. (One day we visited a castle and took photos.)","le passé composé avec être (aller, rester, partir) + accord : Je suis allée à Paris ; nous sommes restés une semaine. (I went to Paris; we stayed a week.)","utiliser … pour + infinitif : J'utilise mon portable pour faire mes devoirs. (I use my phone to do my homework.)","il faut / on doit + infinitif : Il faut recycler plus. (We have to recycle more.)","trop de / moins de / beaucoup de + nom sans article : Il y a trop de déchets. (There is too much rubbish.)","comparaisons de lieux : plus / moins + adjectif + que ; aussi … que : La campagne est aussi belle que la côte. (The countryside is as beautiful as the coast.)","je voudrais + infinitif : Je voudrais retourner à Nice. (I would like to go back to Nice.)"]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-26T20:57:24.964Z","by":"claude-code-hand","models":["claude-fable-5-1","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"hand-written-2026-09-26-fr","judge":{"score":0.82,"notes":"Every answer key re-solves correctly and the French throughout (tenses, agreements, trop de/moins de without article, mark weightings for the 50/90/150-word tasks) is sound; the fixes needed are cosmetic — an inconsistent connector in the first worked example, a slightly overloaded final step, the over-broad il faut/on doit fact, and the missing photo-card style task."}}},"3.2.1":{"id":"AQA-8652","topic":"3.2.1","family":"language","lesson":{"why":"This room is the Foundation tier grammar: the noun phrases, verb phrases, adjectives, adverbs, prepositions and reading-only word patterns you must understand and produce across every theme. Every sentence you write in French depends on these forms — a wrong article, a missing agreement or a tense in the wrong place changes or breaks the meaning — and every reading text uses them to carry meaning. Learn the patterns here as rules with one example each, then check every sentence you write against them: gender and number agreement, negation around the verb, the right auxiliary in the perfect tense, and de after quantities and negatives.","idea":[{"code":"3.2.1.1","h":"Foundation tier noun phrases","t":"Feminine person nouns: add -e (un ami → une amie), or no change with only the article changing (un élève → une élève); -eur becomes -euse or -rice depending on the word listed (un vendeur → une vendeuse, un acteur → une actrice); -en becomes -nne (un Italien → une Italienne). Plurals: add -s to most nouns; add -x to masculine nouns in -(e)au and -eu (un château → des châteaux, un jeu → des jeux); nouns already ending in -s or -x do not change (un bus → des bus). Highly frequent irregulars are simply listed (œil/yeux, monsieur/messieurs). The infinitive can be a noun where English uses -ing: Nager est bon pour la santé (Swimming is good for your health). Articles agree for gender and number (le, la, les; un, une, des), le and la shrink to l' before a vowel or h muet, and French keeps the definite article where English drops it: La santé est importante (Health is important); le mercredi (on Wednesdays). An adjective with the definite article becomes a noun: le seul (the only one), l'anglais (English, the language), l'Anglaise (the Englishwoman, with a capital). Partitive articles (du, de la, des) mark part of a whole — jouer du piano, faire de la natation — and shrink to de after a negative verb and after expressions of quantity: je n'ai pas de frère, beaucoup d'amis. Other determiners: demonstrative ce, cet, cette, ces; possessives mon/ma/mes, ton/ta/tes, son/sa/ses, notre/nos, votre/vos, leur/leurs; interrogative quel, quelle, quels, quelles; indefinite chaque, plusieurs, même(s), autre(s), tout(e)(s)/tous, quelque(s). Pronouns: subject pronouns je … elles, including on; singular direct object pronouns me, te, vous, le, la and indirect me, te, vous, lui go before the verb (Elle me parle ; je lui réponds); reflexive pronouns me, te, se, nous, vous, se also go before the verb, and plural reflexives can be reciprocal (nous nous écrivons — we write to each other); me, te, le/la and se shrink to m', t', l', s' before a vowel; emphatic moi and toi follow prepositions (avec moi, chez toi); the relative pronoun qui is the subject of the clause it opens: la fille qui habite ici."},{"code":"3.2.1.2","h":"Foundation tier verb phrases","t":"Negation wraps the verb: ne … pas, ne … jamais, ne … rien, ne … personne (Je ne mange jamais de viande ; je ne vois personne). Questions can be made by intonation with subject-verb order, with a wh-word (qui, quand, quoi, pourquoi, comment, combien, où) after the verb, by wh-word + est-ce que + subject-verb, or by wh-word + verb-subject: Tu habites où ? / Où est-ce que tu habites ? / Où habites-tu ? Inflectional morphology covers regular -er verbs, the -ir clusters patterning on choisir, partir, venir and ouvrir, the -re clusters on entendre, prendre and traduire, and the four very high frequency irregulars aller, avoir, être and faire, in all six persons for: the present (simple and ongoing, and with a time adverb for the future: je pars demain), the perfect tense (j'ai fini, je suis parti(e), with participle agreement after être and the irregular participles été, eu, fait), the near future aller + infinitive, and the imperative for tu and vous only (Finis tes devoirs ! Allez au lit !). The imperfect is required in the singular only, for habits and ongoing states: j'avais, il était, elle faisait — était also means 'was' before an adjective and avait means 'had'. Nine further irregular verbs (boire, connaître, courir, croire, écrire, recevoir, rire, suivre, voir) are needed in the singular present, with their participles listed. Impersonal verbs: il y a / il y avait / il y aura, il fait + adjective and weather phrases (il pleut), il faut + infinitive, and il est for the time. Modal verbs devoir, pouvoir, savoir and vouloir take an infinitive in all six persons, and je voudrais / il voudrait give 'would like to'. Reflexive verbs follow the same tense rules as every other verb: je me lève, nous nous couchons, elle s'est levée."},{"code":"3.2.1.3","h":"Foundation tier adjectival phrases","t":"Adjectives agree with their noun for gender and number. Gender: add -e (grand → grande); no change if the masculine already ends in mute -e (calme); -x → -se (heureux → heureuse); -el → -lle (naturel → naturelle); -en → -nne (italien → italienne); -f → -ve (sportif → sportive); -er → -ère (premier → première); frequent irregulars such as bon/bonne, nouveau/nouvelle and travailleur/travailleuse are listed. Number: add -s; no change for a masculine already ending in -s or -x (gris, heureux); -al → -aux in the masculine (national → nationaux), with beau/beaux and nouveau/nouveaux listed. Most adjectives follow the noun (une voiture rouge), but the small listed set — petit, grand, bon, nouveau, jeune, vieux, beau, premier and a few more — goes before it: un petit chien blanc. Comparisons use plus … que, moins … que and aussi … que with an adjective (Il est plus grand que moi ; elle est aussi gentille que sa sœur), with plus … de and moins … de as the listed alternatives; the irregular comparatives meilleur(e)(s) (better) and pire(s) (worse) are listed: Ce film est meilleur que le livre."},{"code":"3.2.1.4","h":"Foundation tier adverbial phrases","t":"Adverbs and adverbial phrases of time, manner, frequency and place are listed in the vocabulary list, and their position matters. Short adverbs of frequency and manner normally follow the verb: Je vais souvent au cinéma ; il parle bien français ; in the perfect tense they sit between the auxiliary and the participle: J'ai toujours aimé la musique. Adverbs and phrases of time and place usually go at the start or the end of the sentence: Demain, je vais au marché ; je travaille ici le samedi. Adverbs compare with the same structures as adjectives — plus … que, moins … que, aussi … que: Elle court plus vite que moi ; il parle aussi bien que son frère — and the irregular mieux (better) is listed: Tu chantes mieux que moi (You sing better than me), not plus bien."},{"code":"3.2.1.5","h":"Foundation tier prepositions","t":"Some verbs and adjectives need à or de before a noun or a following verb, and where the preposition changes the meaning the vocabulary list gives both: arriver (to arrive) but arriver à (to manage to); commencer à + infinitive, essayer de + infinitive, content de + infinitive. Places take en or à: en before a feminine country or region (en France, en Bretagne) and à before a city (à Lyon); other listed cases such as au Canada follow the list. À and de contract with the definite article to match gender and number: à + le = au, à + les = aux, de + le = du, de + les = des, while à la, à l', de la and de l' stay separate: je vais au marché, je viens du collège, le prix des livres. De shows possession where English uses 's: le sac de Léa (Léa's bag), la voiture de mes parents. Pour and sans go directly before an infinitive: pour apprendre le français (in order to learn French), sans parler (without speaking)."},{"code":"3.2.1.6","h":"Foundation tier derivational morphology (Reading only)","t":"For reading only, a text may use a word derived by a listed pattern from a listed base — or the base of a listed derived form — so you need to recognise the patterns even though you will not have to produce them. Ordinal numbers add -ième to the cardinal, dropping a final -e first: deux → deuxième, quatre → quatrième; premier/première are irregular and listed. The prefixes in- and im- give the opposite where English has un-/in-: possible → impossible, sécurité → insécurité, également → inégalement. Suffixes that change the part of speech: -able/-eable turns a verb stem into an adjective where English has -able/-ible (porter → portable, changer → changeable); -ion/-ation turns a verb stem into a noun where English has -ion/-ation (progresser → progression, préparer → préparation); -ment turns an adjective into an adverb where English has -ly, added to the feminine form (première → premièrement) or replacing -ant/-ent with -amment/-emment (courant → couramment, patient → patiemment). Any derived form needed for listening or for you to produce is listed separately unless it is obviously recognisable from its base."}],"examples":[{"title":"Feminine and plural noun forms","setup":"Give the feminine of un vendeur and un ami, and the plural of un château and un bus.","steps":["vendeur ends in -eur, and the listed feminine for this word swaps -eur for -euse: une vendeuse.","ami takes the regular feminine -e: une amie.","château ends in -eau, so the plural adds -x, not -s: des châteaux.","bus already ends in -s, so it does not change: des bus. Answer: une vendeuse, une amie ; des châteaux, des bus."],"cues":["Which ending does vendeur have, and what does the feminine become?","Which regular rule gives the feminine of ami?","What plural ending does a noun in -eau take?","What happens to a noun already ending in -s, and what are all four answers?"]},{"title":"Negation and the article after a negative","setup":"Translate into French: 'I don't eat meat and I never drink coffee.'","steps":["'I don't eat' wraps the verb in ne … pas: je ne mange pas.","After a negative verb the partitive article becomes de: je ne mange pas de viande.","'I never drink' uses ne … jamais around bois, and the same de rule applies: je ne bois jamais de café.","Full sentence: Je ne mange pas de viande et je ne bois jamais de café."],"cues":["Where do the two parts of ne … pas go?","What happens to the article before viande after a negative verb?","Which two-part negative means 'never', and does the de rule still apply?","What is the complete sentence?"]},{"title":"The perfect tense with être and with avoir","setup":"Translate into French: 'She went to the cinema and she saw a film.'","steps":["'She went' is aller, which forms its perfect tense with être: elle est allée — the participle takes -e to agree with elle.","'To the cinema' needs à + le, which contracts to au: au cinéma.","'She saw' is voir, which takes avoir and has the irregular participle vu, with no agreement: elle a vu un film.","Full sentence: Elle est allée au cinéma et elle a vu un film."],"cues":["Which auxiliary does aller take, and does the participle change for elle?","What does à + le become?","Which auxiliary does voir take, and what is its participle?","What is the complete sentence?"]},{"title":"Adjective agreement and position","setup":"Translate into French: 'My new friend (a girl) is very sporty and she has a small white dog.'","steps":["'My new friend', a girl, needs ma and the feminine nouvelle, which goes before the noun: ma nouvelle amie.","'Sporty' agrees with the girl: sportif becomes sportive.","'A small white dog' has one adjective from the before-the-noun set and one ordinary colour adjective after: un petit chien blanc.","Full sentence: Ma nouvelle amie est très sportive et elle a un petit chien blanc."],"cues":["Which possessive and which form of nouveau go with a feminine noun, and where does the adjective sit?","What happens to sportif for a girl?","Which of the two adjectives goes before chien and which after?","What is the complete sentence?"]},{"title":"Prepositions with places, contractions and possession","setup":"Translate into French: 'I live in France, in Lyon, and I go to the market with my mother's brother.'","steps":["'In France' uses en before a feminine country, and 'in Lyon' uses à before a city: en France, à Lyon.","'To the market' is à + le marché, which contracts to au marché.","'My mother's brother' is expressed with de: le frère de ma mère.","Full sentence: J'habite en France, à Lyon, et je vais au marché avec le frère de ma mère."],"cues":["Which preposition goes before a country, and which before a city?","What does à + le become before marché?","How does French express 's possession?","What is the complete sentence?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What is the plural of un jeu, and why?","a":"des jeux — masculine nouns ending in -eu take -x."},{"q":"Which verbs take être in the perfect tense, and what happens to the participle?","a":"Verbs of movement such as aller, partir, venir, arriver, rester (and reflexives); the participle agrees with the subject: elle est partie."},{"q":"What is the feminine of heureux and of premier?","a":"heureuse (-x → -se); première (-er → -ère)."},{"q":"Give the irregular comparative adverb for 'better' and use it in a sentence.","a":"mieux: Tu chantes mieux que moi."}]},"room":{"facts":["Plurals: -s for most nouns; -x for masculine nouns in -(e)au and -eu (châteaux, jeux); no change for -s and -x (des bus).","After a negative verb and after expressions of quantity the indefinite and partitive articles (un, une, des, du, de la) become de: pas de viande, beaucoup d'amis; the definite article stays (je n'aime pas les maths).","Perfect tense: avoir + participle for most verbs (j'ai fini, j'ai fait); être + participle with agreement for aller, partir, venir, arriver, rester and reflexives (elle est allée).","Adjective gender: add -e; -x → -se; -el → -lle; -en → -nne; -f → -ve; -er → -ère. Most adjectives follow the noun; petit, grand, bon, nouveau, jeune, vieux, beau go before it.","Contractions: à + le = au, à + les = aux, de + le = du, de + les = des; en before a feminine country, à before a city."],"questions":[{"q":"Translate into French: 'She sings better than me, and I often go to concerts.'","a":"Elle chante mieux que moi, et je vais souvent aux concerts.","sol":"Elle chante mieux que moi, et je vais souvent aux concerts. mieux is the irregular comparative adverb (not plus bien); the frequency adverb souvent follows the verb; à + les contracts to aux.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["'Better' describing how she sings is an adverb — which irregular form does French use?","Where does a short frequency adverb sit in relation to the verb, and what does à + les become?","Comparison clause, et, then subject + verb + adverb + place phrase."],"codes":["3.2.1.4","3.2.1.5"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'I don't like maths.'","a":"Je n'aime pas les maths.","sol":"Je n'aime pas les maths. ne … pas wraps aime, with ne shortened to n' before the vowel; the definite article is kept after any negative verb (je n'aime pas les maths, je n'ai pas le livre); it is only the indefinite and partitive articles (un, une, des, du, de la) that become de.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Which two-part negative wraps the verb?","Check which kind of article maths takes, and what a negative does to that kind of article.","Subject, negative around the verb, then the object with its article."],"codes":["3.2.1.2"]},{"q":"Translate into French, using tu: 'Where do you live?'","a":"Où est-ce que tu habites ? (or: Tu habites où ? / Où habites-tu ?)","sol":"Où est-ce que tu habites ? — wh-word + est-ce que + subject-verb order; Tu habites où ? (intonation, wh-word at the end) and Où habites-tu ? (wh-word + verb-subject) are equally correct.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Which wh-word means 'where'?","Which three question patterns can follow a wh-word?","A wh-word, then the verb and its subject in one of the accepted orders, with a question mark."],"codes":["3.2.1.2"]},{"q":"Complete with the correct form of the adjective: 'Ma sœur est très ___ (heureux).'","a":"heureuse","sol":"heureuse — heureux ends in -x, which becomes -se for the feminine, agreeing with sœur.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The adjective must agree with sœur — what gender is that noun?","Look at the last letter of the adjective and recall which letters replace it in the feminine.","One word."],"codes":["3.2.1.3"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'I play the piano and I do swimming.'","a":"Je joue du piano et je fais de la natation.","sol":"Je joue du piano et je fais de la natation. jouer takes the partitive du with a musical instrument; faire takes the partitive de la with a sport.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Which article follows jouer with an instrument, and faire with a sport?","Check the gender of each noun before choosing the partitive.","Verb + partitive + noun, et, verb + partitive + noun."],"codes":["3.2.1.1"]},{"q":"Translate into French (the speaker is a boy): 'Yesterday I did my homework and I went to bed at ten.'","a":"Hier, j'ai fait mes devoirs et je me suis couché à dix heures.","sol":"Hier, j'ai fait mes devoirs et je me suis couché à dix heures. faire has the irregular participle fait with avoir; se coucher is reflexive, so its perfect tense uses être and the participle agrees with the boy speaking (couché).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Both verbs are completed past actions — which tense, and which participle does faire take?","Which auxiliary does a reflexive verb take in that tense?","Time word, first past-tense clause, et, reflexive past-tense clause with the time."],"codes":["3.2.1.2"]},{"q":"Read this text and answer in English: 'Ma mère est plus stricte que mon père, mais elle est aussi plus généreuse. Elle me donne souvent de l'argent pour le cinéma.' How does the writer compare their mother with their father, and what does the mother often do?","a":"The mother is stricter than the father but also more generous; she often gives the writer money for the cinema.","sol":"plus stricte que mon père — stricter than my father; aussi plus généreuse — also more generous; elle me donne souvent de l'argent pour le cinéma — she often gives me money for the cinema (me is the indirect object pronoun before the verb).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Find the comparison with plus … que, then the second plus later in the sentence.","The object pronoun before donne tells you who receives the money.","Two English answers: the comparison, then the habit."],"codes":["3.2.1.3","3.2.1.1"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'We are going to visit Spain in July because the weather is nicer than in England.'","a":"Nous allons visiter l'Espagne en juillet parce qu'il fait plus beau qu'en Angleterre.","sol":"Nous allons visiter l'Espagne en juillet parce qu'il fait plus beau qu'en Angleterre. aller + infinitive for the plan; visiter takes a direct object with its article (l'Espagne); the weather uses il fait + adjective, compared with plus … que; en before a feminine country.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Which structure gives 'we are going to'?","Which impersonal phrase describes the weather, and which preposition goes before a country?","Plan clause with the month, then parce que + weather comparison."],"codes":["3.2.1.2","3.2.1.5"]},{"q":"Translate into English: 'Il faut manger cinq fruits et légumes par jour, mais je ne mange jamais de légumes.'","a":"You have to eat five fruit and vegetables a day, but I never eat vegetables.","sol":"You have to eat five fruit and vegetables a day, but I never eat vegetables. il faut + infinitive is the impersonal obligation; ne … jamais is 'never', with de replacing the article after the negative.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Which impersonal phrase opens the sentence, and what does it express?","Find the two-part negative around the second verb.","One complete English sentence with two clauses."],"codes":["3.2.1.2"]},{"q":"Read this text and answer in English: 'Nous avons voyagé rapidement en train, et le deuxième jour nous avons visité un musée impossible à oublier.' How did they travel, and on which day did they visit the museum?","a":"Quickly, by train; on the second day.","sol":"rapidement is the adverb from rapide (-ment added to the adjective, 'quickly'); le deuxième jour is 'the second day' (deux + -ième); impossible is in- added to possible. These derived forms can appear in a reading text because their bases are listed.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Two of the words are built from smaller words you know — find the base of each.","Two suffixes are at work: one makes an adverb, the other makes an ordinal number.","Two short English answers."],"codes":["3.2.1.6"]},{"q":"Choose the correct form for each gap from the three given: 'Mes parents ___ au marché le samedi (vais / vont / allez). Tu ___ tes devoirs ? (finis / finit / finissons). Nous ___ le bus (prends / prend / prenons).'","a":"vont ; finis ; prenons","sol":"vont — the ils/elles form of aller for mes parents; finis — the tu form of finir (choisir pattern); prenons — the nous form of prendre.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Match each verb to the person of its subject before looking at the options.","Which ending does each verb pattern take for that person?","Three verb forms, in order, separated by semicolons."],"codes":["3.2.1.2"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'She speaks to me every day and I answer her.'","a":"Elle me parle tous les jours et je lui réponds.","sol":"Elle me parle tous les jours et je lui réponds. parler à and répondre à take an indirect object, so the pronouns are me and lui, placed before the verb.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Both verbs take à before the person — which kind of object pronoun does that need?","Where does an object pronoun go in relation to the verb?","Subject + pronoun + verb + time phrase, et, subject + pronoun + verb."],"codes":["3.2.1.1"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'My brother's bike is faster than my bike, but less comfortable.'","a":"Le vélo de mon frère est plus rapide que mon vélo, mais moins confortable.","sol":"Le vélo de mon frère est plus rapide que mon vélo, mais moins confortable. Possession uses de (le vélo de mon frère); plus … que and moins compare the adjectives, which stay masculine to agree with vélo.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["How does French express 's possession?","Which two comparison structures are needed, and what do the adjectives agree with?","Possessive phrase + verb + comparison, mais + second comparison."],"codes":["3.2.1.5","3.2.1.3"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'I live in a small town in the north of England. My parents work in a hospital and they are very tired in the evening. Last weekend we went to the seaside and it was great. Next year I am going to learn Spanish.'","a":"Four French sentences — key checks: une petite ville (adjective before a feminine noun); travaillent and fatigués agree with ils; nous sommes allés + c'était for last weekend; je vais apprendre for next year.","sol":"J'habite dans une petite ville dans le nord de l'Angleterre. Mes parents travaillent dans un hôpital et ils sont très fatigués le soir. Le week-end dernier, nous sommes allés au bord de la mer et c'était génial. L'année prochaine, je vais apprendre l'espagnol. Key points: dans + une petite ville (petit before the noun, feminine); travaillent for the ils form; fatigués agrees with ils; nous sommes allés (aller with être, plural agreement); c'était (imperfect for description); je vais apprendre (aller + infinitive); l'espagnol with its article.","m":10,"d":3,"hints":["Each sentence sits in a different time frame — decide which before translating it.","For each noun, check gender, article and any adjective agreement before you write it.","Four French sentences in the same order as the English."],"codes":["3.2.1.2","3.2.1.5","3.2.1.1","3.2.1.3"]},{"q":"Write a paragraph of about 90 words in French (the writer is a girl) about your family, covering: (1) a description of one family member with at least two adjectives; (2) what you did together last weekend; (3) what you are going to do together next summer.","a":"About 90 words: two adjectives agreeing with the family member, perfect-tense events with avoir and être for last weekend (nous sommes allées, nous avons acheté), and nous allons + infinitive for next summer.","sol":"Ma mère s'appelle Claire. Elle est petite et très travailleuse, mais elle est aussi un peu stricte. Nous nous entendons bien parce que nous aimons les mêmes choses : la musique et le sport. Le week-end dernier, nous sommes allées au centre commercial et nous avons acheté des vêtements pour mon frère. Le soir, nous avons regardé un film à la maison et nous avons mangé une pizza. L'été prochain, nous allons visiter l'Italie parce que ma mère adore la cuisine italienne, et moi, je vais apprendre à faire des pâtes. (91 words: adjectives agreeing with the person, the perfect tense with avoir and être for last weekend, and aller + infinitive for the plan.)","m":15,"d":3,"hints":["Which structures give a description, a completed past event and a plan?","Check every adjective against the gender of the person, and every past participle against its auxiliary.","About 90 words in three short parts in the order of the bullets."],"codes":["3.2.1.3","3.2.1.2"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'The first day, we saw nobody at the beach, but the girls who live next door arrived at midday.'","a":"Le premier jour, nous n'avons vu personne à la plage, mais les filles qui habitent à côté sont arrivées à midi.","sol":"Le premier jour, nous n'avons vu personne à la plage, mais les filles qui habitent à côté sont arrivées à midi. premier is the irregular ordinal; ne … personne wraps the perfect tense with personne after the participle; qui opens a subject relative clause; arriver takes être and the participle agrees with les filles (arrivées).","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["'Nobody' is a two-part negative — where does its second part go in the perfect tense?","Which relative pronoun links 'the girls' to what they do, and which auxiliary does arriver take?","Time phrase, negative perfect-tense clause, mais, subject + relative clause + perfect-tense verb + time."],"codes":["3.2.1.2","3.2.1.1"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What does à + les become?","a":"aux"},{"q":"Give the perfect tense of 'she went' (aller).","a":"elle est allée"},{"q":"What do un, une, des, du and de la become after ne … pas?","a":"de (or d'): je n'ai pas de frère."},{"q":"Give the feminine of italien and of nouveau.","a":"italienne ; nouvelle"}]},"cards":[{"front":"un château / des châteaux","back":"a castle / castles (-eau takes -x in the plural).","code":"3.2.1.1","open":false},{"front":"un vendeur / une vendeuse","back":"a shop assistant (m / f).","code":"3.2.1.1","open":false},{"front":"me, te, lui (avant le verbe)","back":"to me, to you, to him/her — indirect object pronouns before the verb. Je lui parle.","code":"3.2.1.1","open":false},{"front":"ne … jamais / ne … personne","back":"never / nobody. Je ne vois personne.","code":"3.2.1.2","open":false},{"front":"elle est allée / il est parti","back":"she went / he left (perfect tense with être; the participle agrees).","code":"3.2.1.2","open":false},{"front":"j'ai été / j'ai eu / j'ai fait","back":"I was / I had / I did, made (irregular participles été, eu, fait).","code":"3.2.1.2","open":false},{"front":"je voudrais + infinitif","back":"I would like to + verb. Je voudrais visiter Paris.","code":"3.2.1.2","open":false},{"front":"il faut + infinitif","back":"you have to, it is necessary to. Il faut manger des légumes.","code":"3.2.1.2","open":false},{"front":"heureux / heureuse","back":"happy (-x becomes -se in the feminine).","code":"3.2.1.3","open":false},{"front":"meilleur(e) / pire","back":"better / worse (irregular comparative adjectives). Ce film est meilleur que le livre.","code":"3.2.1.3","open":false},{"front":"mieux","back":"better (irregular comparative adverb). Tu chantes mieux que moi.","code":"3.2.1.4","open":false},{"front":"au / aux ; du / des","back":"to the / of the (à + le, à + les; de + le, de + les). Je vais au marché.","code":"3.2.1.5","open":false},{"front":"en France, à Paris","back":"in / to France (en + feminine country); in / to Paris (à + city).","code":"3.2.1.5","open":false},{"front":"deuxième / premier","back":"second (deux + -ième) / first (irregular ordinal).","code":"3.2.1.6","open":false},{"front":"rapide → rapidement","back":"quick → quickly (adverb in -ment, reading only).","code":"3.2.1.6","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulaire : verbes fréquents et leurs participes","items":["être (été) - to be","avoir (eu) - to have","faire (fait) - to do, to make","aller (allé) - to go","prendre (pris) - to take","voir (vu) - to see","venir (venu) - to come","partir (parti) - to leave","choisir (choisi) - to choose","ouvrir (ouvert) - to open","boire (bu) - to drink","écrire (écrit) - to write","devoir (dû) - to have to","pouvoir (pu) - to be able to","vouloir (voulu) - to want"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulaire : déterminants, adjectifs et adverbes","items":["ce / cet / cette / ces - this, that / these, those","mon / ma / mes - my","leur / leurs - their","quel / quelle - which, what","chaque - each, every","plusieurs - several","tout / toute / tous / toutes - all, every","petit / grand / bon - small / big / good (go before the noun)","nouveau / jeune / vieux / beau - new / young / old / beautiful (go before the noun)","souvent - often","toujours - always","vite - quickly","ici / là - here / there","premier / première - first"]},{"kind":"grammar","title":"Grammaire : les structures de Foundation tier","items":["Féminin des noms : -e, -eur → -euse / -rice, -en → -nne. Ma sœur est vendeuse. (My sister is a shop assistant.)","Pluriel des noms : -s ; -eau / -eu → -x ; -s / -x sans changement. Nous avons visité deux châteaux. (We visited two castles.)","Négation : ne … pas / jamais / rien / personne + de. Je ne bois jamais de café. (I never drink coffee.)","Questions : intonation, est-ce que, inversion. Où est-ce que tu habites ? (Where do you live?)","Passé composé : avoir ou être + participe (accord avec être). Elle est allée au cinéma et elle a vu un film. (She went to the cinema and saw a film.)","Futur proche : aller + infinitif. Je vais apprendre l'espagnol. (I am going to learn Spanish.)","Imparfait (singulier) : j'avais, il était, elle faisait. Quand j'étais petit, j'avais un chien. (When I was small, I had a dog.)","Impératif (tu, vous) : Finis tes devoirs ! Allez au lit ! (Finish your homework! Go to bed!)","Modaux + infinitif : Je dois partir ; nous pouvons rester. (I have to leave; we can stay.)","Accord de l'adjectif : -e, -x → -se, -f → -ve, -er → -ère ; position. Une petite maison blanche. (A small white house.)","Comparatif : plus / moins / aussi … que ; meilleur, mieux. Elle court plus vite que moi. (She runs faster than me.)","Prépositions : en + pays, à + ville ; au / aux / du / des ; de possessif. Le sac de Léa est au collège. (Léa's bag is at school.)","Verbes pronominaux : je me lève, nous nous écrivons. Ils se regardent. (They look at each other.)"]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-26T20:57:24.967Z","by":"claude-code-hand","models":["claude-fable-5-1","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"hand-written-2026-09-26-fr","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"Every answer key I re-solved (all sixteen, including the four-sentence and 90-word models) is correct and matches its solution; the only real publication blockers are the two long-answer 'a' fields that give marking notes instead of a checkable answer and the uneven mark/difficulty weighting on the short translations."}}},"3.2.2":{"id":"AQA-8652","topic":"3.2.2","family":"language","lesson":{"why":"This room is the Higher tier grammar: the extra pronouns, negatives, tenses and structures that sit on top of the Foundation forms and that separate a Higher tier answer from a Foundation one. They let you say what you have just done, what you are in the middle of doing, how long something has been going on, what will happen and what would happen, and which thing is the best or the worst. Learn each as a form with one example, then check your own writing for them: y and en, ne … plus and ne … que, the inflectional future, the conditional of -er verbs, plural imperfects, superlatives with de, and avant de / après avoir.","idea":[{"code":"3.2.2.1","h":"Higher tier noun phrase grammar: articles, determiners, pronouns, relative clauses","t":"Partitive articles go with uncountable nouns (du courage, de la patience); dans keeps the article (dans la ville) while en drops it (en ville, en voiture). The negative determiner aucun(e) means 'no / not any': Il n'y a aucun problème ; je n'ai aucune idée. The pronouns y (there, to it) and en (of it, of them, some) go before the verb and are never stacked with another object pronoun, except in il y en a / il y en avait / il y en aura: J'y vais demain ; j'en ai deux. Plural direct object pronouns nous, vous, les and plural indirect nous, vous, leur go before the verb, one at a time: Il nous voit ; elle leur parle. Emphatic pronouns lui, elle, nous, vous, eux, elles stand alone or follow prepositions: avec eux, chez elle, Lui, il est sympa. Plural reflexive pronouns nous, vous, se also go before the verb: nous nous levons, ils se lavent. The negative subject pronouns personne ne and rien ne open a sentence for 'nobody' and 'nothing': Personne ne parle ; rien ne change. Relative clauses use où for a place or time (la ville où j'habite, le jour où il est parti) and que for a direct object (le film que j'ai vu)."},{"code":"3.2.2.2","h":"Higher tier verb phrase grammar: negation, passive, impersonal verbs, periphrastic expressions, reflexives and inflectional morphology","t":"Negation adds ne … plus (no longer), ne … ni … ni (neither … nor), ne … pas encore (not yet) and ne … que (only), and ne … que keeps the article: Je ne bois que de l'eau. The passive in the present uses être + past participle with par: Le repas est préparé par mon père. Impersonal phrases take de before an infinitive: il est difficile / facile / interdit de + infinitive; and il manque + noun, il vaut mieux + infinitive, il vaut la peine de + infinitive are set phrases. Two time expressions: être en train de + infinitive (to be in the middle of doing) and venir de + infinitive (to have just done). Plural reflexives can be reciprocal: nous nous écrivons, ils se regardent. Two more verb clusters, patterning on connaître and écrire, are needed in the Foundation forms. depuis + present tense gives 'have been doing for': J'apprends le français depuis cinq ans. Modals form a perfect tense with their listed participles: j'ai dû, il a pu, nous avons voulu. The inflectional future of regular -er verbs (je visiterai, nous visiterons) means both 'will' and 'going to', with the singular forms of avoir, faire, aller and être listed (aurai, ferai, irai, serai). The conditional of -er verbs in all six persons (je visiterais, nous visiterions) has the same four irregular singulars (aurais, ferais, irais, serais). The imperfect extends to the plural (nous allions, vous faisiez, ils avaient, elles étaient) for 'used to' and 'was/were -ing'. New imperatives: sois and soyez from être, and the nous form meaning 'Let's …' (Allons au parc ! Finissons !). The present participle after en gives 'while / by doing': en attendant le bus, with étant, ayant and faisant listed."},{"code":"3.2.2.3","h":"Higher tier adjectival phrases: regular superlative structures","t":"The superlative of an adjective is le / la / les + plus or moins + adjective, agreeing with the noun, and 'in' after a superlative is de: C'est la plus grande ville de France (It is the biggest city in France); les plus beaux jardins de la région. If the adjective normally follows the noun, the whole superlative follows it: le film le plus intéressant de l'année. The irregular forms are listed: le meilleur / la meilleure / les meilleur(e)s (the best) and le pire / la pire / les pires (the worst): C'est le meilleur restaurant du quartier ; c'était la pire journée de ma vie."},{"code":"3.2.2.4","h":"Higher tier adverbial phrases: regular superlative structures","t":"The superlative of an adverb is le plus or le moins + adverb, and it never changes form because adverbs do not agree: Elle court le plus vite ; c'est lui qui parle le moins souvent (She runs the fastest; he is the one who speaks least often). The irregular adverb is listed: le mieux (the best) — Ma sœur chante le mieux de la famille (My sister sings the best in the family) — which you must keep apart from the adjective le meilleur: mieux and le mieux describe how something is done, meilleur and le meilleur describe a thing."},{"code":"3.2.2.5","h":"Higher tier prepositions: avant de and après avoir constructions","t":"Two preposition structures sequence events without a second clause. avant de + infinitive means 'before doing': Avant de partir, je ferme la porte (Before leaving, I close the door); Réfléchis avant de répondre. après avoir + past participle means 'after having done', for verbs that take avoir in the perfect tense: Après avoir mangé, nous avons fait la vaisselle (After eating, we did the washing-up); après avoir fini mes devoirs, je suis sorti. Both structures keep the same subject in both halves of the sentence, which is what makes them work without repeating the subject."},{"code":"3.2.2.6","h":"Higher tier derivational morphology (Reading only): agent nouns","t":"For Paper 3 Reading only, a text may use an agent noun built from a listed verb, or the verb behind a listed agent noun, so you need to recognise the pattern rather than produce it. The suffix -eur (or -ateur) added to a verb stem names the person who does the action: porter → un porteur (a carrier), consommer → un consommateur (a consumer), vendre → un vendeur (a seller), jouer → un joueur (a player). If an agent noun is needed for listening or for you to produce, it is listed separately unless it is clearly recognisable from its base."}],"examples":[{"title":"The pronouns y and en","setup":"Translate into French: 'Are there any shops? Yes, there are some, and I go there every day.'","steps":["'Are there any shops?' is a question by intonation: Il y a des magasins ?","'There are some' replaces des magasins with en, and this is the one place y and en sit together: il y en a.","'I go there every day' replaces the place with y before the verb: j'y vais tous les jours.","Full answer: Il y a des magasins ? Oui, il y en a, et j'y vais tous les jours."],"cues":["How do you ask 'are there any shops?' using intonation?","Which pronoun replaces 'some shops', and where does it go?","Which pronoun replaces 'there', and where does it go?","What is the complete exchange?"]},{"title":"Higher tier negatives: ne … plus and ne … que","setup":"Translate into French: 'I no longer eat meat and I only drink water.'","steps":["'No longer' is ne … plus around the verb: je ne mange plus.","After ne … plus the article shrinks to de, as after ne … pas: je ne mange plus de viande.","'Only' is ne … que, and it is the exception: the article stays — je ne bois que de l'eau.","Full sentence: Je ne mange plus de viande et je ne bois que de l'eau."],"cues":["Which two-part negative means 'no longer'?","What happens to the article after ne … plus?","Which negative means 'only', and does the article shrink after it?","What is the complete sentence?"]},{"title":"venir de and the inflectional future","setup":"Translate into French: 'We have just arrived, and tomorrow we will visit the museum; it will be interesting.'","steps":["'We have just arrived' is venir de + infinitive in the present: nous venons d'arriver.","'We will visit' is the inflectional future of the -er verb visiter: infinitive + -ons gives nous visiterons.","'It will be' uses the listed irregular future of être: ce sera intéressant.","Full sentence: Nous venons d'arriver, et demain nous visiterons le musée ; ce sera intéressant."],"cues":["Which structure gives 'have just' + verb, and what tense is venir in?","How is the future of a regular -er verb formed for nous?","What is the future of 'it is'?","What is the complete sentence?"]},{"title":"Superlatives of an adjective and an adverb","setup":"Translate into French: 'It is the most beautiful city in France, and my sister sings the best.'","steps":["'The most beautiful city' is la plus belle ville — belle before the noun, agreeing with ville.","'In France' after a superlative is de: la plus belle ville de France.","'Sings the best' is the irregular superlative adverb le mieux, which never changes: ma sœur chante le mieux.","Full sentence: C'est la plus belle ville de France, et ma sœur chante le mieux."],"cues":["How is the superlative of an adjective built, and does it agree?","Which preposition follows a superlative for 'in'?","Which irregular form gives 'the best' for a verb, and does it agree?","What is the complete sentence?"]},{"title":"avant de, après avoir and the present passive","setup":"Translate into French: 'Before leaving, we eat; the meal is prepared by my father, and after eating, we do the washing-up.'","steps":["'Before leaving' is avant de + infinitive: avant de partir.","'We eat' and 'we do the washing-up' are plain present-tense main clauses: nous mangeons ; nous faisons la vaisselle.","'The meal is prepared by my father' is the present passive, être + participle agreeing with le repas, and par for 'by': le repas est préparé par mon père.","'After eating' is après avoir + past participle: après avoir mangé.","Full sentence: Avant de partir, nous mangeons ; le repas est préparé par mon père et, après avoir mangé, nous faisons la vaisselle."],"cues":["Which structure gives 'before doing'?","What are the two main clauses in the present tense?","How is the present passive built, and which word gives 'by'?","Which structure gives 'after having done'?","What is the complete sentence?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which pronoun replaces 'there / to it', and which replaces 'of it / some'?","a":"y (J'y vais); en (J'en ai deux)."},{"q":"Which Higher tier negative keeps the article instead of shrinking it to de?","a":"ne … que (only): Je ne bois que de l'eau."},{"q":"Give the future and the conditional of 'she is' (être).","a":"elle sera; elle serait."},{"q":"What is the difference between le meilleur and le mieux?","a":"le meilleur is the superlative adjective (the best thing/person, agreeing); le mieux is the superlative adverb (does something the best, invariable)."}]},"room":{"facts":["y = there / to it; en = of it, of them, some — both before the verb, stacked only in il y en a / il y en avait / il y en aura.","Higher negatives: ne … plus (no longer), ne … ni … ni (neither … nor), ne … pas encore (not yet), ne … que (only — keeps the article); personne ne / rien ne open a sentence.","Inflectional future of -er verbs: infinitive + -ai, -as, -a, -ons, -ez, -ont; irregular singulars aurai, ferai, irai, serai. Conditional: infinitive + -ais, -ais, -ait, -ions, -iez, -aient; aurais, ferais, irais, serais.","Superlatives: le / la / les + plus / moins + adjective (agreeing) + de for 'in'; le plus / le moins + adverb (invariable); irregular le meilleur, le pire, le mieux.","avant de + infinitive = before doing; après avoir + past participle = after having done; venir de + infinitive = to have just done; être en train de + infinitive = to be in the middle of doing."],"questions":[{"q":"Translate into French: 'Nobody speaks.'","a":"Personne ne parle.","sol":"Personne ne parle. personne ne is the negative subject pronoun: personne opens the sentence and ne goes before the verb, with no pas.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["'Nobody' as the subject is a two-word negative — which word opens the sentence?","Where does ne go, and is pas needed?","Two words, then the verb."],"codes":["3.2.2.1"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'My sister runs the fastest.'","a":"Ma sœur court le plus vite.","sol":"Ma sœur court le plus vite. The superlative of an adverb is le plus + adverb, and it never changes form because it describes the verb, not a noun.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The superlative describes how she runs — an adverb, not an adjective.","How is the superlative of an adverb built, and does it agree?","Subject + verb + superlative adverb."],"codes":["3.2.2.4"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'It is the biggest house in the street.'","a":"C'est la plus grande maison de la rue.","sol":"C'est la plus grande maison de la rue. The superlative is la plus grande, agreeing with maison and placed before it because grand goes before the noun; 'in' after a superlative is de.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["How is the superlative of an adjective built, and does it agree with maison?","Which preposition gives 'in' after a superlative?","C'est + superlative + noun + de + place."],"codes":["3.2.2.3"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'Before eating, I wash my hands.'","a":"Avant de manger, je me lave les mains.","sol":"Avant de manger, je me lave les mains. avant de + infinitive gives 'before doing'; se laver les mains is reflexive with the definite article for the body part.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Which structure gives 'before doing'?","Which reflexive verb gives washing a part of yourself, and which article goes before the body part?","Avant de + infinitive, comma, then the reflexive clause."],"codes":["3.2.2.5"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'I have been learning French for five years.'","a":"J'apprends le français depuis cinq ans.","sol":"J'apprends le français depuis cinq ans. depuis + a length of time with the present tense expresses something that began in the past and is still going on; English uses 'have been -ing'.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Which preposition gives 'for' a length of time that is still going on?","Which tense does French use with that preposition?","Subject + present-tense verb + object + depuis + time."],"codes":["3.2.2.2"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'We are in the middle of doing our homework; we have just finished the maths.'","a":"Nous sommes en train de faire nos devoirs ; nous venons de finir les maths.","sol":"Nous sommes en train de faire nos devoirs ; nous venons de finir les maths. être en train de + infinitive gives 'be in the middle of doing'; venir de + infinitive in the present gives 'have just done'.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Which two set expressions give 'be in the middle of doing' and 'have just done'?","Which tense are être and venir in for these expressions?","Two clauses, each with subject + expression + infinitive + object."],"codes":["3.2.2.2"]},{"q":"Read this text and answer in English: 'Je viens de recevoir un message : mes cousins vont arriver demain. Il vaut mieux préparer la chambre ce soir, parce qu'il est difficile de tout faire le matin.' What has just happened, and what does the writer think it is better to do, and why?","a":"They have just received a message; their cousins are arriving tomorrow; it is better to prepare the room this evening, because it is difficult to do everything in the morning.","sol":"je viens de recevoir un message — I have just received a message; mes cousins vont arriver demain — my cousins are going to arrive tomorrow; il vaut mieux préparer la chambre ce soir — it is better to prepare the room this evening; parce qu'il est difficile de tout faire le matin — because it is difficult to do everything in the morning.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The opening phrase says something has just happened — find it.","The impersonal phrases il vaut mieux and il est difficile de carry the advice and the reason.","Three short English answers: the news, the advice, the reason."],"codes":["3.2.2.2"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'She speaks better than me, but my brother speaks the best.'","a":"Elle parle mieux que moi, mais mon frère parle le mieux.","sol":"Elle parle mieux que moi, mais mon frère parle le mieux. mieux is the irregular comparative adverb, le mieux its superlative; both are invariable because they describe the verb, not a noun.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["'Better' describing how someone speaks is an adverb — which irregular form?","How is the superlative of that adverb built?","Comparison clause, mais, then subject + verb + superlative adverb."],"codes":["3.2.2.4"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'He gives them a present every year, and they thank him.'","a":"Il leur donne un cadeau chaque année, et ils le remercient.","sol":"Il leur donne un cadeau chaque année, et ils le remercient. donner à takes the plural indirect object pronoun leur; remercier takes a direct object, so 'him' is le; both go before the verb.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["'Gives to them' needs an indirect object pronoun — which plural form?","'Thank him' takes a direct object — which pronoun, and where does it go?","Subject + pronoun + verb + object + time phrase, et, subject + pronoun + verb."],"codes":["3.2.2.1"]},{"q":"Translate into English: 'Il n'y a aucun problème : il est interdit de fumer ici, mais on peut manger en attendant le train.'","a":"There is no problem: smoking is forbidden here, but you can eat while waiting for the train.","sol":"There is no problem: it is forbidden to smoke here, but you can eat while waiting for the train. aucun is the negative determiner 'no'; il est interdit de + infinitive is an impersonal phrase; en attendant is the present participle after en, 'while waiting'.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["aucun is a negative determiner — what does it do to 'problem'?","Two structures to spot: an impersonal phrase with de + infinitive, and en + a present participle.","One complete English sentence with a colon and a mais clause."],"codes":["3.2.2.1","3.2.2.2"]},{"q":"Read this text and answer in English: 'Les consommateurs achètent moins de plastique, et les vendeurs proposent maintenant des sacs en papier.' Who is buying less plastic, and what are the sellers now offering?","a":"Consumers; paper bags.","sol":"consommateurs is the agent noun from consommer (consumer); vendeurs from vendre (seller); des sacs en papier — paper bags. Agent nouns in -eur / -ateur can appear in a reading text when their base verb is listed.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Two nouns name people who do an action — find the verb inside each.","Which suffix turns a verb into the person who does it?","Two short English answers."],"codes":["3.2.2.6"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'We used to go to the beach every summer and we were very happy.'","a":"Nous allions à la plage tous les étés et nous étions très heureux.","sol":"Nous allions à la plage tous les étés et nous étions très heureux. The plural imperfect gives 'used to' (nous allions) and 'were' + adjective (nous étions), with heureux agreeing with nous.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["'Used to' and 'were' describe the past as a habit and a state — which tense, in the nous form?","Does heureux need an ending for nous?","Two clauses joined by et, both in the same past tense."],"codes":["3.2.2.2"]},{"q":"Translate into French (the speaker is a girl): 'When I was younger, we used to live in the countryside and we were very happy. Now I have been living in Paris for two years. I only go back to the village at Christmas. Next year I will leave school and I would like to study languages, because it is easier to find a job with two languages.'","a":"Quand j'étais plus jeune, nous habitions à la campagne et nous étions très heureux. Maintenant, j'habite à Paris depuis deux ans. Je ne retourne au village qu'à Noël. L'année prochaine, je quitterai le collège et j'aimerais étudier les langues, parce qu'il est plus facile de trouver un travail avec deux langues.","sol":"Quand j'étais plus jeune, nous habitions à la campagne et nous étions très heureux. Maintenant, j'habite à Paris depuis deux ans. Je ne retourne au village qu'à Noël. L'année prochaine, je quitterai le collège et j'aimerais étudier les langues, parce qu'il est plus facile de trouver un travail avec deux langues. Key points: nous habitions / nous étions (plural imperfect); depuis + present (j'habite … depuis deux ans); ne … que for 'only'; je quitterai (inflectional future of the -er verb quitter) and j'aimerais (conditional of the -er verb aimer) + infinitive; il est plus facile de + infinitive.","m":10,"d":3,"hints":["Sort the sentences by time: past habit, present state that started earlier, present, future with a wish.","Match each English structure to its French form before writing: used to, have been -ing for, only, will, would like.","Five French sentences in the same order as the English."],"codes":["3.2.2.2"]},{"q":"Write a paragraph of about 90 words in French about your town, covering: (1) how long you have lived there and what it is like; (2) what people used to do there in the past; (3) what will change next year.","a":"e.g. J'habite à Lille depuis dix ans. C'est une grande ville du nord, et à mon avis c'est la plus agréable de la région parce qu'il y a beaucoup de parcs. Quand mes grands-parents étaient jeunes, les gens travaillaient dans les usines et ils n'avaient pas de voiture ; ils allaient au travail à pied tous les jours. Aujourd'hui, il n'y a plus d'usines dans le centre. L'année prochaine, la ville organisera des concerts dans le parc et on plantera des arbres dans ma rue. Je pense que ce sera mieux pour tout le monde, surtout pour les jeunes.","sol":"J'habite à Lille depuis dix ans. C'est une grande ville du nord, et à mon avis c'est la plus agréable de la région parce qu'il y a beaucoup de parcs. Quand mes grands-parents étaient jeunes, les gens travaillaient dans les usines et ils n'avaient pas de voiture ; ils allaient au travail à pied tous les jours. Aujourd'hui, il n'y a plus d'usines dans le centre. L'année prochaine, la ville organisera des concerts dans le parc et on plantera des arbres dans ma rue. Je pense que ce sera mieux pour tout le monde, surtout pour les jeunes. (98 words: depuis + present, a superlative with de, plural imperfects for the past, and the inflectional future for the changes.)","m":15,"d":3,"hints":["Which structures give how long you have been somewhere, what people used to do, and what will happen?","Pair each bullet with one tense, and add an opinion with a reason at the end.","About 90 words in three short parts in the order of the bullets."],"codes":["3.2.2.2","3.2.2.3"]},{"q":"Translate into French (the speaker is a boy): 'The town where I was born is the prettiest in the region; after visiting the centre, my friends wanted to stay.'","a":"La ville où je suis né est la plus jolie de la région ; après avoir visité le centre, mes amis ont voulu rester.","sol":"La ville où je suis né est la plus jolie de la région ; après avoir visité le centre, mes amis ont voulu rester. où opens the relative clause about the place; la plus jolie … de is the superlative with de; après avoir + participle gives 'after visiting'; vouloir forms its perfect tense with the listed participle voulu.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Which relative pronoun gives 'where', and which structure gives 'after doing'?","How is the superlative built, and what is the perfect tense of vouloir?","Relative clause + superlative, semicolon, après avoir clause + perfect-tense clause."],"codes":["3.2.2.1","3.2.2.3","3.2.2.5","3.2.2.2"]},{"q":"Translate into French: 'Let's go to the park! Nothing is more relaxing than a walk, and there is no traffic there.'","a":"Allons au parc ! Rien n'est plus relaxant qu'une promenade, et il n'y a aucune circulation là-bas.","sol":"Allons au parc ! Rien n'est plus relaxant qu'une promenade, et il n'y a aucune circulation là-bas. The nous imperative gives 'Let's'; rien ne is the negative subject pronoun; aucune is the negative determiner agreeing with circulation.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Which imperative form gives 'Let's …'?","'Nothing' as a subject and 'no traffic' both use Higher tier negatives — which?","Imperative + place, then a comparison clause, et, a negative il y a clause."],"codes":["3.2.2.2","3.2.2.1"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Give the French for 'I have just eaten'.","a":"Je viens de manger."},{"q":"What does ne … pas encore mean?","a":"not yet"},{"q":"Give the future of 'we visit' (visiter) and of 'I go' (aller).","a":"nous visiterons; j'irai"},{"q":"Translate: 'après avoir fini mes devoirs'.","a":"after finishing (having finished) my homework"}]},"cards":[{"front":"j'y vais","back":"I go there (y = there / to it, before the verb).","code":"3.2.2.1","open":false},{"front":"j'en ai deux","back":"I have two of them (en = of it / of them / some).","code":"3.2.2.1","open":false},{"front":"leur (avant le verbe)","back":"to them — plural indirect object pronoun. Je leur parle.","code":"3.2.2.1","open":false},{"front":"aucun / aucune","back":"no, not any. Il n'y a aucun problème.","code":"3.2.2.1","open":false},{"front":"la ville où …","back":"the town where … (où = relative pronoun of place / time).","code":"3.2.2.1","open":false},{"front":"ne … plus / ne … que","back":"no longer / only. Je ne bois que de l'eau.","code":"3.2.2.2","open":false},{"front":"venir de + infinitif","back":"to have just done. Je viens de finir.","code":"3.2.2.2","open":false},{"front":"être en train de + infinitif","back":"to be in the middle of doing. Nous sommes en train de manger.","code":"3.2.2.2","open":false},{"front":"depuis + présent","back":"have been doing for. J'habite ici depuis dix ans.","code":"3.2.2.2","open":false},{"front":"je visiterai / je visiterais","back":"I will visit / I would visit (future / conditional of -er verbs).","code":"3.2.2.2","open":false},{"front":"nous allions / ils étaient","back":"we used to go / they were (plural imperfect).","code":"3.2.2.2","open":false},{"front":"le / la plus … de","back":"the most … in. La plus grande ville de France.","code":"3.2.2.3","open":false},{"front":"le mieux","back":"the best (superlative adverb, invariable). Elle chante le mieux.","code":"3.2.2.4","open":false},{"front":"avant de + infinitif / après avoir + participe","back":"before doing / after having done. Avant de partir ; après avoir mangé.","code":"3.2.2.5","open":false},{"front":"consommer → un consommateur","back":"to consume → a consumer (agent noun in -eur / -ateur, reading only).","code":"3.2.2.6","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulaire : négations et expressions impersonnelles","items":["ne … plus - no longer","ne … ni … ni - neither … nor","ne … pas encore - not yet","ne … que - only","personne ne … - nobody …","rien ne … - nothing …","aucun / aucune - no, not any","il est difficile de - it is difficult to","il est interdit de - it is forbidden to","il vaut mieux - it is better to","il vaut la peine de - it is worth","il manque - there is a lack of, … is missing"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulaire : verbes et formes de Higher tier","items":["j'aurai / j'aurais - I will have / I would have","je ferai / je ferais - I will do / I would do","j'irai / j'irais - I will go / I would go","je serai / je serais - I will be / I would be","sois / soyez - be (imperative of être)","étant / ayant / faisant - being / having / doing","j'ai dû / j'ai pu / j'ai voulu - I had to / I could / I wanted","connaître (connu) - to know (a person, a place)","écrire (écrit) - to write","le meilleur / le pire - the best / the worst","le mieux - the best (adverb)","un joueur / une joueuse - a player"]},{"kind":"grammar","title":"Grammaire : les structures de Higher tier","items":["y et en devant le verbe : J'y vais ; j'en veux. (I go there; I want some.)","pronoms pluriels devant le verbe : Il nous voit ; elle leur écrit. (He sees us; she writes to them.)","personne ne / rien ne + verbe : Rien ne change. (Nothing changes.)","relatives avec où et que : le jour où il est parti ; le film que j'ai vu. (the day he left; the film I saw)","ne … plus / ni … ni / pas encore / que : Je n'ai ni frère ni sœur. (I have neither brother nor sister.)","passif au présent : Le repas est préparé par mon père. (The meal is prepared by my father.)","venir de / être en train de + infinitif : Je viens de manger ; je suis en train de lire. (I have just eaten; I am reading.)","depuis + présent : Elle habite ici depuis deux ans. (She has lived here for two years.)","futur simple des verbes en -er : Nous visiterons Paris. (We will visit Paris.)","conditionnel des verbes en -er : Je visiterais Paris si j'avais le temps. (I would visit Paris if I had time.)","imparfait au pluriel : Nous allions à la plage ; ils étaient contents. (We used to go to the beach; they were happy.)","impératif : Sois sage ! Allons-y ! (Be good! Let's go!)","participe présent après en : Il chante en travaillant. (He sings while working.)","superlatif : la plus belle ville de France ; elle court le plus vite. (the most beautiful city in France; she runs the fastest)","avant de + infinitif ; après avoir + participe : Avant de partir, après avoir mangé. (Before leaving; after eating.)"]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-26T20:57:24.967Z","by":"claude-code-hand","models":["claude-fable-5-1","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"hand-written-2026-09-26-fr","judge":{"score":0.83,"notes":"Every answer key re-solves correctly and the French is accurate throughout; the fixes needed are cosmetic — align the key and solution wording on the reading translation, soften the hint that names aucun as a negative, and either use or drop the '(the speaker is a girl)' cue in the long translation."}}}} };
